The prime ministers of India and Japan met in Tokyo.
India's new Prime Minister Narendra Modi is meeting his Japanese counterpart Shinzo Abe in Toko on his first major foreign visit since his election victory in May to discuss economic and security relations.
Mr. Modi is on a five-day trip to Japan consolidating economic relations with the world's third-largest economic nation.
Plans for greater nuclear cooperation are at the top of the agenda.
India also reportedly is hoping for a treaty on defense cooperation between the two nations.
Karratha police arrest 20-year-old after quick motorcycle hunt
A motorcycle was seized after the rider drove it at 125 km/h in a 70 km/h zone and through bushland to escape police in Bilbara.
Traffic police in Karratha attempted to stop a blue motorcycle this morning after watching it leave a petrol station on Bathdate Road at 125 km/h.
Police report that the driver then ignored the holding signals and continued driving on Burgess Road, before turning into the bushland, where officers lost sight of it.
The motorcycle as well as a person who matched the driver's description were later seen at a house in the Walcott Way in Bulgarra.
Karratha's police accuses a 20-year-old man of failing to pay attention to a holding signal as well as ruthless driving.
He is due to appear before the District Court in Karratha on September 23.
The motorcycle was secured and seized for three months.
George Webster charged with hotel rapes in Naim and Pitlochry
A man is on trial for raping women in two hotels.
George Webster, 28, was readied the indictment at a hearing by Glasgow's Supreme Court.
He is accused of raping a woman at Scotland's Hotel in Pitlochry in Perthshire on June 7, 2013.
The indictment is that Webster assaulted her while she "was unconscious, slept and couldn't signal consent."
Webster is also accused of raping a second woman at the Golf View Hotel in Naim in the Scottish highlands on May 4, 2014.
Judge Lady Rae scheduled the trial date for November 17 at Edinburgh's Supreme Court.
Recalling back to the very American ideal of labour rights as human rights
Congressmen Keith Ellison and John Lewis have tabled a legislative proposal to establish the organization of unions as a civil right.
"Just as unions are dying, the middle-class jobs are also dying," said Ellison, a Democrat from Minnesota and vice chairman of the Progressive Caucus in Congress.
That’s why I proudly present the employee empowerment law together with civil rights icon John Lewis.
This groundbreaking law gives workers the same legal options in the event of discrimination because of the organization of trade unions as in other forms of discrimination - and thus halts anti-union-hired forces.
Complementing the National Labour Law Act to create an opportunity for discrimination-suspended workers to organize a trade union representation, to sue for justice in a civil court - and to receive damages or fines - is a sensible and necessary initiative.
But it's almost certainly not a radical initiative - at least not according to American standards.
In fact, the best way to understand what Ellison, Lewis and the other sponsors of their bill are suggesting is the connection back to a very American idea.
Despite the setbacks faced by unions in recent years - in Wisconsin, Michigan, and other states across the country - Americans have once encouraged countries around the world to recognize, expand and uphold labor rights.
There was a time that millions of Americans still remember when this country called democracy, freedom of speech, freedom of the press, and the right of unification.
When the United States occupied Japan after World War II, General Douglas Mac Arthur and his staff encouraged the country to pass a constitution designed to ensure that the militarized autocracy Hedki Tojos would be replaced by democracy.
In full awareness that the workers and their unions played a role in the creation of a new Japan, they used a language that explicitly acknowledged that "the rights of the workers to unification and joint bargaining and action are guaranteed."
When the United States occupied Germany after World War II, General Dwight David Eisenhower and his staff urged the Germans to write a constitution that would ensure that Adolf Hitler's fascism would be replaced by a strong democracy.
Recognising the fact that the workers would have to organize and raise their voices in the new nation, the Germans incorporated a clause that explicitly stated: "The right to establish unions in order to secure and improve the working and economic conditions is guaranteed to every individual and every profession or trade.
Agreements restrictinging or aiming to reduce it are null and void; measures aimed at this are unlawful.
When former First Lady Eleanor Roosevelt presideded over the International Commission on Human Rights, of which the Universal Declaration of Human Rights was drafted, as adopted by the United Nations in 1948 as a worldwide agreement, Roosevelt and the other authors attached a guarantee that "everyone has the right to form or accede to trade unions to protect their interests."
For generations, Americans have been accepting the basic premise that labor rights are human rights.
When this country advised other countries on how to shape civil and democratic societies, Americans declared that the right to establish a trade union - and that this union should then be allowed to participate in collective bargaining as an equal partner with corporations and government agencies.
Since these rights came under fire in America, it is actually very wise to revisite the American ideal, according to which the working population must have the right to unite and make themselves heard in a free and open society.
As Pastor Martin Luther King Jr. said fifty years ago:
The story is a great teacher.
Now everyone knows that the workers' movement did not limit the strength of the nation, but magnified it.
By raising the living standards of millions of people, Labor has miraculously created a market for the industry and raised the entire nation to unimaginable levels of production.
Those who attack the work forget these simple truths, but history preserves them.
The story remembers, and that's what we should.
Formal recognition of labour rights as human rights - and expanding the protection of citizens' rights as protections against discrimination in the creation of workers' representations - is long overdue.
Keith Ellison and John Lewis are renewinging the ideals that have made America great in the past and made the promise of democracy.
Judice halts temporary law that all abortion clinics in Louisiana could force closure
A U.S. federal judge provisionally halted the implementation of a law in Louisiana on Sunday that lawyers say it presumably led to the closure of all five abortion clinics in the state.
This measure, which was brought to law by Bobby Jindal, governor of Louisiana, in June and is due to come into effect on Sept. 1, would force doctors who perform abortions to acquire a patient retarial right at a hospital within a radius of 30 miles around their practice.
However, the judge's decision means the doctors will be able to provisionally continue to legally perform abortions while applying for such a right.
"The plaintiffs may continue to work in accordance with the law as they strive to obtain these rights," Federal Judge John deGravelles wrote in his ruling.
Within a month, a hearing is scheduled for the judge to bring about a more permanent decision for the law.
Abortion advocates welcomed the decision, the latest in a series of rulings against similar measures, and said doctors were thus gaining more time to gain hospital admission rights.
"Today's decision means women in Louisiana are escapinating a devious law that seeks to deprive them of their health and rights," said Nancy Northup, president and chairwoman of the Center for Reproductive Rights, who had sued on behalf of three of the state's five clinics to halt the law.
It was not immediately clear whether the verdict also extended to the doctors from the two clinics, who had not joined the lawsuit and also applied for admission rights.
Louisiana is one of 11 states that have enacted similar laws, with courts recently declaring such measures in Alabama and Mississippi non-constitutional.
Key passages of a law in Texas that would have forced most of the clinics remaining in the state to close were halted by a federal judge on Friday.
Abortion advocates and the American College of Ostretricians and Gynecologists, as well as the American Medical Association, consistently, say admission rights from doctors require unnecessary restrictions.
Opponents of abortion counter that the measures serve to protect women's health, although some also welcomed the effects of a closure of clinics.
Only one doctor who performs abortions in Louisiana has patient admission rights, the Center for Reproductive Rights said.
If all other doctors in the state are forced not to perform abortions anymore, this doctor would no longer carry out the operation out of fear for his safety, the group said.
In their argument against the verdict, representatives of the state of Louisiana said they would not punish doctors for performing abortions, during whose requests for patient admission rights would still be pending.
Delayed diagnoses and lack of access to the best treatment options lead to higher death rates from ovarian cancer in rural areas.
Angelina Jolie and her brother James posted a video homage for their mother who died of ovarian cancer in 2007.
For women in the rural areas of Australia, there is a higher risk of ovarian cancer than for women in cities.
Researchers analyzed the medical data from more than 1100 Australian women diagnosed with ovarian cancer in 2005 and found that only 25 percent were still alived five years after diagnosis.
Research leader Susan Jordan of the QIMR Berghofer Medical Research Institute said that for those living in rural and remote regions of the state, the mortality risk during the study was 20 percent higher than for those in urban areas.
SMALL STUDY: New drugs could slow lung and ovarian cancer
The researchers examined women's medical care over seven years.
Dr Jordan said a woman's age at diagnosis, the type of ovarian cancer, existing pre-existing conditions, as well as social and economic status, affected the chances of survival.
For older women and those whose cancer was already more advanced at the time of diagnosis, the survival rate was lowest.
For those who lived in rural and remote regions of the state, the risk of mortality during the study was 20 percent higher than for those in urban areas.
Although the study didn't investigate why women living outside the city were more likely to die from ovarian cancer, Dr. Jordan suspects a belated diagnosis and lack of access to the best treatment centers as possible factors for this.
"This disease is best treated by gynecological oncologists, and these are mostly found in larger cities," she said.
Despite the improvement of telemedicinal services in order to oppose the distances, she proposed as a possible remedy reinforcement of the service of flown-in physicians to give specialists the opportunity to provide women closer to their place of residence, as well as treatment programs for people outside their region.
Dr. Jordan said that regardless of geographical status, the study revealed a poor long-term survival rate among women in ovarian cancer and underlined the need for better treatment and preventive strategies.
The study, funded by Rio Tinto Ride to Conquer Cancer, will be published today in the Medical Journal of Australia.
In March 2013, Elisha Neave, a young mother of 33, learned from the Gold Coast that she had an aggressive form of ovarian cancer.
Garden centres regret declining homeowner numbers
The decline, along with an intensified decline in the number of homeowners under the age of 35, could result in gardening centres losing tens of thousands of pounds a year if today's young consumers, according to an HTA study, achieve the "nuclear age group for garden products."
The report says tenants spend an average of 55 per cent of the amount that people with homeownership spend on their garden.
The rise of people living in highly urbanized areas without gardens, the popular process of paving front yards for parking purposes and the declining garden size are further factors that threaten the industry where an estimated €5bn is implemented in sales annually.
In the greater London area, where the proportion of homeowners has fallen from 61 to 43 per cent over a period of 6 years, the lowest spending on garden products per household is reported.
The HTA and the Royal Horticultural Society point out that housing for rent or small gardens doesn't mean people can't grow crops.
Guy Barter, chief garden adviser for the RHS, said: "Container gardeners, for example, are very popular with tenants because they can take their plants with them on a move."
The HTA report identified the period between 1997 and 2005 as the "golden age" of garden retail due to rising homeowners and economic prosperity from the late 1980s to the mid-90s.
He also predicted an improved market situation this year because of the better weather after unfriendly weather conditions in March and April last year.
The protest movement in Pakistan led by opposition politician Imran Khan and cleric Tahirul Qadri continues.
In the capital Islamabad, around 1000 protesters have stormed the state-run television channel PTV.
Information Minister Pervaiz Rashid said soldiers could have pushed the protesters out of PTV buildings again.
The transmitter ceased operations for about 40 minutes, but now broadcasts again.
At the same time, around 3000 protesters attempted to arrive at Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif's residency.
About a kilometre away, they delivered collisions with security forces equipped with sticks and stones.
The police used tear gas.
The protesters demanded Sharif's resignation, but he rejects it.
The country's army, meanwhile, calls for a political solution to the conflict "without wasting time and using without violence."
After a meeting at the headquarters on Sunday evening, the military said: "The army remains committed to its role to ensure the security of the state."
The military plays an important role in Pakistan and has already coupped more frequently.
Opposition politician Imran Khan accuses Premier Sharif of cheating in May last year's general election.
The protests had escalated over the weekend after Khan and Qadri called on their supporters to advance to Sharif's residency.
Three people were killed.
Turkey appoints U.S. diplomat over espionagency report
Turkey's Foreign Ministry has summoned the country's longest-serving U.S. diplomat for information on a report on American and British espionage in Turkey.
Deputy Prime Minister Bülent said the U.S. embassy executive and Turkish officials had discussed the report Monday.
The German magazine Der Spiegel, as well as the online magazine The Intercept, reported that by former U.S. analyst at the National Security Agency, Edward Snowden, shown that Turkey was a major area of activity for U.S. and U.S. intelligence agencies.
According to Turkish news agencies, Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan downplayed the importance of the report and said all the major countries spied on each other.
An earlier report that Germany's most important intelligence service was also targeting Ankara caused much more disgruntled reactions from the Turkish government.
Warning strike: Engine drivers threaten consequences for commuters
The nationwide warning strike of the train drivers on Monday evening could also impair passenger traffic in NRW.
The stand-off, announced for 6 p.m. to 9 p.m., affects all transport companies nationwide, including long-distance, regional and suburban rail traffic.
The maining direction of the warning strike is freight traffic.
Photograph: A. Burgi/Archive
The district chairman of the trade union German locomotive drivers (GDL), Sven Schmitte, said in Düsseldorf.
We expect significant failures.
There are no regional priorities.
The main thrust of the warning strike is freight traffic.
Deutsche Bahn in NRW announced it would do everything possible to keep the impact as low as possible.
However, the GDL did not say where it wanted to strike, so it was difficult to foresee the consequences.
Magaluf police chief charged with corruption
Prosecutors on Friday submitted evidence of extortion by police officers and employees of the city of Calvià to the office of the prosecutor of Mallorca.
Calvià's chief of police on the holiday island of Mallorca has been arrested following corruption allegations by businessmen and bar owners in the notorious Binge drinking hotspot Magaluf.
Chief Commissioner José Antonio Navarro has been taken into pre-trial detention by several businessmen from Punta Ballena after allegations of corruption against him by several businessmen from Punta Ballena, the street where most of Magaluf's bars and nightclubs lie.
According to the online daily Mallorca Diario, prosecutors submitted evidence of extortions by police and employees of the city of Calvià to the prosecutor's office on Friday.
Two other local police officers have been arrested by Spain's Guardia Civil in connection with the corruption allegations and are being questioned jointly with Navarro by a judge.
The national Spanish daily ABC reported that the disgruntled night-club owners testified that the authorities' nepotism had a serious impact on their businesses.
"It's not about making money anymore, it's about survival," one of the businessmen told the court.
Leave our livelihood out of the game.
There's nothing to lose.
Magaluf made international headlines this summer due to a viral YouTube video showing an 18-year-old British tourist who performed Fellatio on 24 men during a drinking tour.
The island authorities have since attempted to stop the disorderly behaviour of alcohol-fueled holidaymakers in Magaluf by minimizing the number of participants in the notorious alcohol-soaked pub tours.
The Playhouse Club, where the Fellatio incident took place, has been forced to close for a year, while Playhouse and pub tour organizer Carnage will collectively have to pay €55,000 ($73,000).
The tourist town Magaluf, which is especially popular with young British holidaymakers, is also known for several accidents under the influence of alcohol, including the trend of "balconing", in which people jump from one balcony to another or from a balcony into the hotel pool.
The first day of spring with wet and stormy weather conditions affects flights from Adelaide Airport
SPRING has given southern Australia a wintry surprise, with heavy showers and strong wind affecting flights from Adelaide airport.
In the evening until 9 p.m., another 5mm rain fell on the city, after 6.6mm that had already gone down in the night to Sunday.
The last precipitation was the result of a series of short, stormy showers, including a casting that started just before 8pm and caused nearly 4mm of rainfall in about 10 minutes.
After the winter caused an early dose of spring last week, temperatures in the city fell back to a high of just 15.8°C on Monday.
The gusty weather conditions have likely led to the delayed landing of a Virgin Airlines flight from Melbourne to Adelaide.
The plane should have landed shortly after 7.30 p.m. but was hit by a shear wind - a sudden change in wind speed or direction over a short distance - and had to take off.
Gusts of wind reached about 50 km/h on the ground at Adelaide airport at that time.
The flight data showed that the aircraft also had to take off during a second landing approach and finally set off at about 20.40.
The flight delays subsequently led to the delay of some other flights, including an Emirates flight from Dubai and a quantas flight from Sydney.
A maximum temperature of 16°C is predicted for Adelaide on Tuesday, with possible showers.
Matthies says it's high time for big networking
In view of the galloping technical progress that threatens to transform our comfortable home into high-tech control panels, our columnist Bernd Matthies consoles himself with an exceedingly familiar electronic accessory.
As I write this here, my gaze falls on the spiral cable that connects the listener to the phone, and I think: Boah, how retro is that?
But there is a consideration behind it: if the cable were not, in a very notice of time, all listeners would fly around the houselessly, and that would be much more retro.
In any case, applies on the ekeltronic (ekeltronic?
No, typos: electronic) Sector the rule that today's world novelty is tomorrow's e-waste bought - obsolete.
And if, for example, you still have a tube TV in your living room today, it merely says that it fundamentally rejects television.
What are these considerations?
Because this week’s pushing of the Ifa at the radio tower once again shows us our absolute incompetence in questions of electronics.
The layman, who formerly mastered the washing machine and CD player, does not even understand the purpose of the devices, which are spreaded in front of him with incredibly vain expert talk: "Connected Home, Wearables, Health Care and Urban Technologies" the press office promises us.
Yeah, isn't we?
What to talk about with people who seem to voluntarily spread such quark?
Basically, for about 20 years it has mainly been about the fact that we should network our house.
So that the yogurt re-orders itself, so that the fire alarm on holiday in the Maldives tells us that it burns in the study.
And wouldn't it be wonderful if we from the office the air conditioning on our soon arrival ...?
Yes, however, this requires air conditioning.
But a dream will definitely come true soon: We lift our wristwatch, tell her out loud "Suck!" - and at home the vacuum robot drives off.
That's crazy.
Everything boils down to nothing less than a revolution: "The boundaries between white and brown goods are blurring," an industry connoisseur whispers, you can connect the washing machine with a headphone, the steamer sends the weather report and the data glasses indicate how long the pizza in the oven still takes, which is of no use, because we are in traffic jams.
We also know from everyday practice: If a new-fashioned device is really urgently needed, the battery is guaranteed empty.
Which is another reason that the spiral cable on the phone will also survive the next radio exhibitions.
Israeli children return to their schools after Gaza war
Thousands of Israeli children from the areas near the Gaza Strip returned to schools on Monday after spending the summer in aerial shelters, while rocket and mortar shelling at their communities during the 50-day war between Israel and Hamas and schools in Gaza remained closed while the area recovered from the fighting.
The school launch brought a sense of joy and expectance to communities drawn by rocket bullet holes in southern Israel, but the signs of the fighting are still fresh.
In the southern city of Ashdod, employees of the "Pashosh" nursery, which was hittered by a rocket, removed shrapnel from the walls and sliding walls before students arrived.
"We're a bit scared, but are excited," said Ronit Bart, a resident at Kibbutz Saad and English teacher there.
Many children from our area urgently need to return to a routine.
Her 11-year-old daughter Shani Bart said it felt "a bit weird" to suddenly go back to school.
"These were difficult times, and we didn't go out of the house at all," she said.
President Reuven Rivlin visited kibbutz near the border with Gaza to offer his support.
Until a ceasefire last week, thousands of residents of border communities, such as Saad, remained in their homes or left their homes to move to safer areas farther away from Gaza and escape rocket and mortar fire.
Many residents of Nahal Oz, a community near the Gaza border where a 4-year-old boy was killed by a Palestinian mortain grenade, are still hesitating to return.
The Ministry of Education said about a dozen families had not yet returned.
Their children were provisionally housed in other schools.
Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu visited a school in Sderot, a border town with Gaza heavily drawn by Palestinian shelling.
He appealed to the children to learn a lot and said "we're going to make sure that you acquire knowledge and provide you with security."
Israel and Hamas agreed on an indefinite ceasefire last Tuesday.
The ceasefire caused an immediate end to the fighting; however, important problems remain unresolved, such as Hamas's call for a lifting of the Israeli-Egyptian blockade of Gaza and the reopening of air and seaports in Gaza.
Israel calls for a disarmament of Hamas and the handover of the bodies of two Israeli soldiers killed in the fighting.
A new round of indirect talks is expected to begin later this month in Egypt.
More than 2,100 Palestinians were killed in the war, three-quarters of them civilians, and at least 494 children, according to estimates by the Palestinians and the U.N.
Israel denies this information and estimates that at least half of those killed are fighters, but did not provide concrete evidence of this information.
On the Israeli side, 66 soldiers and six civilians, including a Thai worker, were killed.
Hamas and other fighters in Gaza fired 4,591 rockets and mortar shells during the fighting at Israeli cities, most in the country's south.
Meanwhile, the Israeli military carried out more than 5,000 aerial and other attacks.
The Israeli attacks damaged or destroyed thousands of homes in the Gaza area and about 250,000 people sought refuge in more than 100 U.N. schools that have been converted into temporary shelters.
While tens of thousands of people continue to live in the shelters and the struggles continue, education authorities postponed the start of the school year last week.
"I hope the school is open again soon so that we can continue to learn, just like the children in the world and the Jewish children," said Mohammad Amara, a 13-year-old boy who currently resides in a school in Gaza City.
After election in Saxony: CDU deputy Strobl questions NPD prohibition proceedings
In view of the loss of votes of the NPD in Saxony, CDU Federal Deputy Thomas Strobl has advocated an end to the ongoing NPD prohibition proceedings before the Federal Constitutional Court.
According to CDU Federal Deputy Thomas Strobl, the ongoing prohibition proceedings against the NPD before the Federal Constitutional Court should come to an end.
"The NPD performs worse than last time," Strobl, who is also CDU's head of state in Baden-Württemberg, told the "Leipziger Volkszeitung."
This is a pleasing development in the right direction and a development that makes me rather come to the conclusion that the NPD is being fought politically and not before the Constitutional Court.
The NPD had narrowly failed in Sunday's vote at the five percent hurdle.
A first attempt for a ban of the far-right party had failed in 2003, because the constitutional protection at that time also had informants in the NPD leadership.
In December 2013, the Federal Council submitted a new application in Karlsruhe after a long preparation.
The federal government and the Bundestag did not join in.
Harburg: Tents for new refugees
Refugees are now housed in tents at Neuländer Platz.
The central reception for refugees in Harburg is bursting at the seams.
Now the interior authority has set up three Red Cross tents next to it.
The district politicians have only been informed shortly before - they are little thrilled.
"These Hauruck actions continue to stoke citizens' reservations and give district politicians no opportunity to participate constructively in the humane accommodation of refugees," says Britta Herrmann, group leader of the Greens in the Harburg district assembly.
Her party is demanding that the Harburg district administration currently make unused homes available for accommodation.
96 refugees can be accommodateded in the three tents.
Reasons for the overcrowding in the initial reception are the onslaught of help seekers - and the fact that the authorities cannot provide enough places in follow-up facilities.
Therefore, many refugees stay longer than the regular three months.
House prices have experienced the strongest winter rise in seven years, a widely observed index shows.
The RP Data CoreLogic Hedonic Home Value Index for homeownership in the Australian capital rose 1.1 per cent in August, according to Monday's RP data.
As a result of this increase, the total increase in June, July and August is 4.2 percent, the largest increase in the winter months since 2007.
The annual price increase was 10.9 percent, more than twice as high as the increase in the 12 months to August 2013, but the increases were not evenly distributed across the country.
RP Data's head of research, Tim Lawless, shared that Sydney and Melbourne are driving a two-divided market.
RP Data's figures show that home prices in Sydney rose 16.1 per cent last year, while in Melbourne by 11.7 per cent.
The following markets are Adelaide, Brisbane and Darwin, in which prices rose on average by five and six per cent.
At the other end of the scale is Canberra, which has been hit hard by cuts in government spending and where prices rose by only 1.4 percent over the year.
Mr. Lawless went on to say that with the beginning of spring now for the next few months there would be an increase in the properties for sale, which would subject the market "to a real test."
"If you look at the consistently high rate of auction prices, a generally fast selling rate and the consistently low interest rate environment, it's likely that home prices will rise even further in the next three months," he said.
In a statement to the Neue Presse, he contradicts statements by the managing director Peer Medau and attests to posing "like a general".
The style of the university is not, the message said.
In mid-June, for example, the Medau family had already been orally informed about the request of the university.
The statement that the plans only became known in July are definitely wrong," Pötzl said.
At the same time, he points out that the plans are not "pre-determinations or even decisions", but that there were "preferences".
The expansion of the narrow road up to the castle, which is necessary for the access road, is also less dramatic than feared.
A nine-meter-wide road was and is not mentioned.
According to the current situation, it is 5.50 meters, which is one meter wider than before," explains Pötzl.
Pötzl does not want to accept the argument of the Medau operators that their free and school-owned parking spaces will only be used by students of the university in the future.
It is not parked at all, because, as with other public parking lots, this can now be solved intelligently.
Thinking local residents
The crèche Bergwichtel, which is located on the premises and on whose doorstep would lead the driveway along the parking deck, should "not be affected," according to the university president.
Rather, he considers "that this is not only about the crèche, but also about the residents at the junction with Friedrich-Streib-Straße and the 800 students of the neighboring secondary school.
So it's about much more than the particular interests of Mr Medau," says Pötzl.
"Whether the criticism of the parent advisory board of the crèche, with the expansion of the road, one of Coburg's few natural spots would be lost", "the reader can decide for himself".
At the beginning of October, Pötzl, together with the State Building Office Bamberg, intends to inform the city councils about the structural development of the university by 2020 "and in this context address the importance of the new development".
Peer Medau had informed the parents of the crèche last week about the plans of the university.
Accordingly, the entrance for the new parking deck should not lead as before over the campus and Friedrich-Streib-Straße, but over the Medau-Berg.
Thus, the narrow road would have to be two-lane expanded over about one kilometer in length.
According to projections of the Medaus, the traffic in the park of Hohenfels Castle would increase tenfold.
There is talk of about 2000 vehicles per day.
On the part of the city, they did not want to comment on the project.
Glandorf resigns from handball national team
Holger Glandorf will no longer play for the DHB team.
The 31-year-old from the Bundesliga club SG Flensburg-Handewitt cited health problems for this decision.
The decision has not been easy for me, it has matured over several months.
It's time for me to listen to the signals of my body and end my career in the national team," the 2007 world champion shared on his Facebook page.
With a record of 167 international matches and 576 goals for Germany, he is thus retiring internationally.
Because the powerful backroom player for the Champions League winner from Flensburg wants to go on a goal chase for a few more years, he is no longer available to the new national coach Dagur Sigurdsson.
Glandorf had a "long and good conversation with the Icelander, who announced his squad for the international matches against Switzerland on Tuesday.
His ideas and approaches inspired me just as much as they will inspire my long-term teammates and my successors.
But for my club I also want to be available healthy in the next few years, longer regeneration breaks are necessary, which the current double burden does not allow.
The left-hander even had to fear his end of his career in 2012 after a heel infection.
Due to Achilles tendon problems, he had been treated by team doctors of the German Handball Federation (DHB) with a cortisone syringe, which had later triggered severe inflammation.
An emergency operation and two other interventions followed.
Then Glandorf had announced a lawsuit against the team doctor, the dispute ended with a comparison.
Twelve years of professional and eleven years of national team careers do not go past any body without a trace.
I've got some health construction sites that I can't ignore anymore.
After the three infection-related foot surgeries that took place at short intervals in 2012, I never got to grips with my blood pressure properly.
In addition, there are some other wear-related complaints.
Already at the beginning of the year, I realized that I could control my head, but I can't deceive my body," Glandorf explained in an interview to Handball Time magazine.
For a comeback, he would only be ready in an absolute emergency.
"When all the ropes break, I'm always ready to talk," the two-time family man explained to the trade magazine.
However, I can count the years in which I can still play handball on one hand.
I also have to say that I also became quite humbled as far as my health after my long hospital stay in 2012.
After my career, at least I want to be able to gone straight.
In addition to the 2007 World Cup title and winning the Champions League in 2014, he won the EHF Cup in 2008 (HSG Nordhorn) and 2010 (TBV Lemgo).
In 2012 he also secured the European Cup Winners’ Cup with Flensburg.
German economy shrunk by 0.2 percent in spring
Crises around the globe slowed down the German economy in the second quarter.
Gross domestic product fell 0.2 percent from April to the end of June 2014 compared to the previous quarter.
This was announced by the Federal Statistical Office and thus confirmed preliminary calculations.
Consumption alone revived the economy in the spring, while investment declined.
Foreign trade also slowed the economy.
Thanks to the strong start to the year, there is an overall GDP increase of 0.8 percent for the first half of 2013 compared to the second half of 2013.
Horstmar was the starting point and destination of the "Münsterland-Mooiste", which led for the first time through Burgmannsstadt on Sunday.
The Burgmann town of Horstmar witnessed an invasion of Dutch "Fietzenfahrer" on Sunday.
The largest cycling federation of the Netherlands, NTFU, hosted the so-called "Münsterlande-Mooiste".
This is the largest cycling tour across Münsterland, the cyclist paradise near the Netherlands.
More thanked Jos ter Huerne with their registration for the organization of this tour through the Münsterland.
This tour was offered to interested parties in various trade journals.
We come from Den Helder and drove overnight to take part in this tour.
We would also like to get to know Münsterland better in this way," the cyclists were pleased about the route through the districts of Steinfurt, Borken and Coesfeld.
