Konstantin Wecker's ruthlessly honest memoirs
It's actually more philosophical testimony than biography what Konstantin Wecker, songwriter, pacifist, political activist, ex-detainee, put on paper in "Mönch und Krieger" on 287 pages.
Not chronologically, but more shreds of thought shreds of thoughts, the Munich-born on June 1, 1947, has written down his memoirs, each chapter initiated through a suitable song text.
And he’s ruthlessly honest about it: from onanie as a high school student to shoplifting and drug use, he doesn’t leave out any human weakness.
Also to point out how he himself learned tolerance in dealing with his fellow human beings.
Wide space also occupy abstract, but all the more important terms: love, soul, ego, being in the now, spirituality - all this he tries to get to the bottom, in a very philosophical, but also very personal way.
And he also explains his pacifism, his social criticism, his political commitment - and also how he not only shaped the music (the opera lover became a soul and rock musician), but also his imprisonment.
The latter has also brought the lifeman Konstantin Wecker closer to the ascetic monk, which arises "by subtraction," as he says "by omitting everything unnecessary."
Between all the high-spirited views, however, there are also short anecdotes to the earth: for example about brawls in a restaurant frequented by GIs or an insane car journey, during which he frontally targeted a tree.
There, too, he is ruthlessly honest.
Lenny Henry: My dad never took me in his arms.
