Houthi rebels black roads in Yemen's capital
In the conflict between Shiite Houthi rebels and the Yemeni government, the Houthis have called for civil disobedience in the capital Sanaa.
Houthi supporters had occupied central streets in Sanaa on Monday, said a spokesman for the Houthi party "Ansar Allah."
Admission to parliament has also been blocked.
Yemeni media outlets are reporting a traffic chaos in the capital.
According to the online edition of the newspaper "Yemen Post," Houthi leader Abdulmalik al-Huthi had called for the measures on Sunday night.
Houthi threatened a further escalation of the conflict, according to the paper, should his demands not be met.
The Shiite Houthis are fighting for more independence from Sunni-ruled Yemen and are demanding the resignation of the government.
Houthi's people's tribune has besieged the Yemeni capital for almost two weeks.
Around 30,000 rebels have set uprising protest camps on the major access roads of Sanaa.
In the surrounding area, bloody battles with the Yemeni army occur again and again.
Kenya registers civil servants to curb 'ghost workers'
Kenya has begun to biometrically register all state officials to delete "ghost employees" from the government's payrolls.
Employees who do not register in the next two weeks would no longer be paid, according to a government statement.
The government suspects that thousands of people will still receive salary payments even after their departure from the civil service.
President Uhuru Kenyatta pledged to fight corruption in the civil service when he took office in 2013.
In an audit earlier this year, it was found that at least $1 million (£700,000) per month was lost to payments for "ghost workers" and through other abusive financial practices.
The government suspect salaries will continue to be paided to accounts after a person has died or is leaving public service, Wanyama Chebusiri reports for the BBC from the capital Nairobi.
All officials must now appear at identification centres over the next two weeks to ensure their data about the biometric registrations are recorded, it has been communicated in a government news.
All those who would not do so without a credible apology would be removed from the payroll, the message continued.
"Through this approach will contribute significantly to the rationalization of the public service by establishing the actual number of civil servants and also cleaning up the payroll at both levels of government - this is how "ghost employees" are eliminated," said Anne Waiguru, the cabinet secretary in the Ministry of Decentralization and Planning.
The scandal surrounding art consultant Helge Achenbach will be heard in court for the first time on 11 November.
Five heirs of Berthold Albrecht, son of Aldi founder Theo Albrecht, are claiming 19.4 million euros in damages from Achenbach and two of his companies, which are in bankruptcy proceedings, in a civil case at the Düsseldorf Regional Court.
This was announced by a court spokeswoman on Monday.
The lawsuit states that Achenbach cheated on the purchase of artworks and vintage cars and Berthold Albrecht charged higher than actually paid purchase prices.
As a result, the agreed commission was also higher.
Achenbach has bought valuables for Albrecht for 121 million euros.
Children need roots and wings
He spent 44 years in school service, 23 of them as headmaster.
From this time, Manfred Richter knows many confessed Petershausen and Kollbacher from their time as pupils.
His methods were sometimes unconventional, but always supported by his striving to see man in the student.
Schneekoppe is the most well-known mountain in the Giant Mountains.
Manfred Richter loves the painting with the motive from his homeland.
Richter was born in 1930 in Trautenau/Nordböhmen, today Trutnov.
Already at the age of 14, he was interested in the teaching profession after four years in high school.
One year later, the Sudeten Germans were expropriated.
Within half an hour, the family had to leave their house.
After a few months in a camp we went to Bavaria in 1946 - in the freight wagon.
That's how Richter moved to the Upper Franconian Senkendorf.
Teacher training took place in Bamberg.
To do this, Manfred Richter had ten kilometers six times a week to the nearest station, take the train to Bamberg, go there three kilometers across the entire city to school and back in the evening.
After two months, Manfred Richter was given a place to sleep in a Red Cross home in Bamberg: together with up to 15 classmates in a dormitory.
In 1951 he took the first and in 1954 after three years teacher training candidate the second state examination.
Since Oberfranken had no vacant teacher position for him, he applied to Upper Bavaria and came to the school in Kollbach on 1 November 1954.
The "Fräulein room" was his first "service apartment" in the schoolhouse.
In 1958, he moved into a three-bedroom service apartment in the newly built teacher's house next to the school.
This house was bought by Manfred Richter in 1980, after the school in Kollbach was dissolved in 1968.
Until then, there were three classes in Kollbach in the Petershausen-Kollbach school association and five in Petershausen.
From the school year 1968/69, these were summarized in Petershausen in today's town hall.
When the school association Indersdorf was founded, only the elementary school remained in the village.
In order to avoid a downgrading in the now smaller school, the then rector Georg Sigl moved to Karlsfeld - and Manfred Richter became his successor.
But Petershausen grew, and so the children and their teachers were able to move into today's construction from September 1982, which was expanded in 1997 after Richter's retirement.
Manfred Richter praises the uncomplicated generosity of the then mayor Ludwig Götz, who also had a factory room built at municipal costs and during the expansion in 1997, although this was not eligible according to the guidelines for school buildings.
"Mayor Götz was very school-friendly," Richter recalled gratefully.
In his entire career, Manfred Richter has never forgotten the speech of a government representative who had responded to a presentation by a school council on law and justice: "You must never forget love." Manfred Richter implemented what he had belonged as a young teacher.
"You have to give the children two things: roots and wings," says the 84-year-old.
And he had to find out: "That doesn't always succeed." Whether he means only his students or his own six children is not quite clear.
In any case, Richter's actions were sometimes unconventional.
When pupils fought, they didn’t get punishment from him and weren’t condemned to detention either.
At the expense of the municipality, Richter had two pairs of boxing gloves purchased and let the two opponents compete against each other, even if there should be a violet.
For the benefit of the school, Richter maintained a good relationship as rector with the then mayor Ludwig Götz (CSU).
He persuaded the non-partisan judge after his retirement to run at least as the last on the CSU list for the local council in 1996.
But Richter was pre-elected at number six and joined the CSU.
180 kilometers in three days
HR4-RADTOUR 700 cyclists make stopovers in the spa park of Bad Salzhausen and Scots
If cyclists stop on their tour in Bad Salzhausen or Schotten, that in itself is nothing special.
On Friday, however, only the number of restings made an impression: The 700 participants of the hr4 bike tour and accompanying tross enjoyed a break in the lower spa park of Bad Salzhausen and later an open-air party in Schottener Alteburgpark.
Under the motto "Cycling and celebrating on newly discovered paths between Nidda and Kinzig", the participants had met on Thursday in Bad Vilbel and witnessed a concert by Van Baker & Band there.
On Friday it became serious: the cyclists tackled the first stage, the 60 kilometers via Florstadt to Bad Salzhausen and on to Schotten.
In Bad Salzhausen, the athletes were greeted by Mayor Hans-Peter Seum, who had co-started in Bad Vilbel, and Petra Schwing-Döring.
For the refreshment of body and mind, all sorts of good things had been prepared: The rural women from Ranstadt and Nieder-Mockstadt had baked 85 tins of apple cakes, sprinkle, sprinkle and cheesecakes, which mouthed wonderfully to the capable cyclists.
"Such cakes can only bake rural women," praised a participant from Aulendiebach, while ordering another piece.
Cold refreshments could be bought at the drinks stand of the Geiß-Nidda gymnastics club.
The "Duo Bravo" entertained the visitors with well-known melodies from operettas and musicals and received enthusiastic applause.
The beautiful ambience of the park was relaxing.
A gentleman also enjoyed a head massage from his partner: "He deserved it, after all, he has already reattached bicycle chains that have already jumped off".
Meanwhile, the mayor and the company manager still had to work: They were interviewed at the fountain in front of the Trinkkurhaus by reporter Rainer Janke and took the opportunity to advertise Bad Salzhausen on the radio.
Tobias Hagen, head of promotion Tobias Hagen, since the first hr4 cycling tour in 2004, was only able to breather for a short breath, because he had plenty to do with the technology on site.
His pick-up truck, in which he communicated by means of three telephones and a radio with his employees and the 15-strong motorcycle squadron of the Hessian police, wrote Facebook entries and made reports, also needed a check.
"The majority of the participants come from the Rhine-Main area," he recounted, "but also cyclists have arrived from Gießen or Fulda, from Lauterbach or the Taunus and even from numerous cities in Westphalia.
The number of participants depends a bit on the available overnight capacities.
In category I, the participants are accommodated in hotels, in Category II in collective accommodation.
The cyclists had a good impression from the health resort when they left after an hour and a half: "Goodbye!"
"It was beautiful in Bad Salzhausen!" they waved goodbye.
After arriving in Scots, the group was allowed to take a short breather.
But in the evening, the cyclists were challenged in the idyllic Alteburgpark.
Under the direction of the Schottener Gesellschaft für Tourismus und Stadtmarketing (GTS), the park had received a nice festive ambience and, of course, treats, such as flambée with a well-cooleded Feather whitener, had been prepared.
Mayor Susanne Schaab, GTS Managing Director Markus Hoßfeld and MSC Chairman Wolfgang Wagner-Sachs welcomed the visitors in his function as a board member of the ADAC Hessen-Thüringen.
The three were visibly pleased with the "many operations in Scots."
DJ Tobias Hagen then released the dance stage.
The opportunity for lively movement in the open air was eagerily exploited despite the sporting activity on the bike.
Citizens who were just as impressed by the good atmosphere as the large crowd of cyclists.
On the second day for the cyclists, the stations Birstein, Wächtersbach and Bad Orb - including a concert with the Trenkwalders - were on the program.
Yesterday, after 180 kilometers of cycling, the athletes arrived back in Bad Vilbel.
Dozens of Turkish police arrested for 'conspiracy' against government
A total of 33 police officers have been arrested in Turkey on suspicion of "conspiracy against the government," local media outlets report.
The police authorities did not make any immediate commentation.
Among those arrested are 14 high-ranking police officers, Hürriyet Daily News reports.
Some of them were involved in the corruption investigation against government employees, including four government ministers last December.
In July, a number of Turkish police officers were arrested for allegedly setting up an organized criminal association and tapping phone numbers.
Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan (who was prime minister at the time) described their actions as part of the activities spearheaded by Islamist cleric Fethulla Güllen against him and other persons in positions of power.
Not all children in Ukraine return to school
Schools almost everywhere in Ukraine opened on Monday (1 September) after the summer holidays.
This day is traditionally an important day for families, and like thousands of other Ukrainian parents, Prime Minister Arseniy Yatsenyuk brought his daughter to school.
There he told waiting journalists that not all schools had reopened, but that he was determined to defend the country for future generations:
The September celebrations weren't held in every school.
The sky isn't peaceful everywhere over Ukraine.
We have to fight for a peaceful sky.
The whole of Ukraine, a huge common Ukrainian Popular Front must fight for a peaceful sky.
Aleksan Pastukhow, headmaster of the school in Slavyansk, visiting Yatsenyuk's daughter, spoke in Russian.
We hope that there will finally be peace here again and that the children will receive an education that will be useful to them in their future lives.
The first day of the new school year is traditionally celebrated, with the children wearing embroidered shirts, holding balloons in their hands and giving flowers to their teachers.
With Rona Fairhead, the BBC may have found the great director she needs.
She arrives with a proud glory from the banking world, media management and the inner circle of the Tory party.
And she has her own frontline experience.
Her career started at the global management consulting firm Bain and Co., whereupon she joined Morgan Stanley, Bombadier, ICI and Pearson's media world.
She was chief executive of the Financial Times for seven years and resigned when the parent company's top post of Pearsons went to a younger male colleague.
Their severage was reportedly nearly £1 million.
Their political ranking is solid, too.
She was endorsed David Cameron by Lord Browne's former chairman of BP when he was looking for more private-sector knowledge in Whitehall: she became an adviser to the cabinet.
Her husband is a former Tory council member.
Already in May, I described the chairman's task with a poisoned chalice.
Not only does the BBC stand at the heart of public life as a huge and complex entity, but there is also an internal paradox in its structure.
Confidence acts in two directions: inward towards the first guard of one's own management hierarchy in the BBC, but also on the outside as a voice of public concern and unrest when things are going wrong.
This makes for an almost unsustainable duopoly that calls for complete comprehensive reform.
But which newly-incoming chairman would rise to compete, slap into everything, and take apart an institution that is as complex as a Chinese dynasty, while he might put himself around his job.
It's a difficult task.
And as if that weren't enough already, many are still interested in seeing the BBC downsized - power, money and status obsolete.
As the competition is approachinging and new technologies threaten old, convenient collateral, the upcoming negotiation of royalties for the BBC is associated with particular dangers.
For the modest £145.50 per year, the British public is buying into surely the world's largest media company.
The BBC tells a good story: it says it reaches 96 per cent of all households and each of them costs just 40 pence a day.
And apparently, the BBC on the popularity scale is rising again: 53 per cent support it today, compared to 31 per cent 10 years ago.
The viewing and usage habits for the BBC have changed: I'm getting news headlines on my phone these days and I'll see missed programs later on my iPlayer.
But she remains a very beloved and considerable institution.
She needs a considerable chairman - I hope she's found him.
