In the earlier tweet, an unfavorable picture of Travis County District Attorney Rosemary Lehmberg was drawn convicted of drunk driving in April 2013.
Perry vetoed funding from her office when she refused to resignate, leading to an indictment against Perry - who could potentially be 2016 presidential nominee - before a grand jury in Austin this month.
"I don't always drive drunk with the triple allowable level of alcohol ... but when I do, I accuse Governor Perry if he chats it out."
I'm the drunkiest Democrat in Texas.
Lehmberg's office was not entrusted with the leadership of the grand jury's investigation against Perry.
This one was led by Michael McCrum, a special prosecutor from San Antonio, who was selected by a Republican judge.
Perry pleads not guilty and called the indictment a political trick.
His high-class team of lawyers requested the judge entrusted with the case to dismiss the indictment and argued that the law against the longest-serving governor in the history of the state of Texas is unconstitutionally diffuse.
Perry halted $7.5 million of state funds to the state's Public Integrity Unit - which is based in Travis County and is investigating instances of state corruption in Texas - when Lehmberg refused to resignate.
This veto prompted a public complaint on the part of a left-wing watchdog group.
Perry's official user account is frequently updated - and sometimes famous.
After graduating as fifth at the Iowa electoral conventions during his 2012 presidential nomination, Perrry addressed speculation that he was going to stop posting a tweet of a photo of himself jogging near a lake, with the words "South Carolina we're coming!"
Berkeley declares housing market 'normal' again
One of London's most prominent contractors pointed out that the housing market in south-eastern England had "returned" to normal levels.
Homeownerships in the capital have been highly sought-after and the subject of huge price rises, prompting the Bank of England to impose caps on mortgage loans in June due to the widespread fear of a credit bladder.
Tony Pidgley, founder and chief executive of high-class construction firm Berkeley, said on Monday: "Since the start of the current financial year, the market has returned to normal transaction levels since peaking in 2013, adding that this provides a "stable action environment."
The property market in London was well positioned during the downturn as foreign buyers crowded into the capital.
According to information from the cadastral office, prices in the urban area have skyrocketed by 18.5 per cent in the past year alone, far more than the average increase in England and Wales overall.
Average selling prices at Berkeley's private, low-cost and student offerings rose by about a fifth last year and were around £423,000 at the end of April.
A stronger pound, however, has made property ownership in London less attractive to foreign buyers in recent months - some of them have also been deterred by the introduction of new property taxes and political rhetoric surrounding a possible "villa tax" ahead of next May's general elections.
London-based real estate agent Foxtons warned last week that the Mortgage market report for April, which unveiled tougher lending schemes, would also lead to lower market growth in both real estate sales as well as prices in the second half of the year.
New data from the Bank of England from Monday shows a decline in July mortgage approval, which also suggests that the property market is cooling.
Hamptions International, another real estate agency, corrected its prediction for the rise in property prices in London downwards to 3 per cent for 2015, based on an already weakening house price awareness.
Transaction volume in London's most expensive areas, such as Chelsea, Mayfair and Kensington, has meanwhile declined by a quarter year after year, according to broker WA Ellis.
Nevertheless, the desire for a homeownership in the capital is a boon for Berkeley, as the amounts due for futures sales amount to more than £2.2 billion.
Mr Pidgley added: "Demand for the right, well-planned product at the best locations remained stable and as a result, futures sales have also remained stable."
In June, the company reported selling 3,742 new homeownerships in the year by the end of April - nearly a third more than at its peak in 2007 before the crisis.
Annual pre-tax earnings rose 40 per cent annually to £280m, while sales rose 18 per cent to £1.6 billion.
Speaking in a note Monday before the company's annual meeting, Mr Pidgley said revenues for Berkeley in the current year were expected to meet current market expectations.
The analysts consistently estimate the annual pre-tax result at £450 million.
Berkeley shares stood firmly at £23.96 in London trading in the afternoon.
