Lesotho's military member says no coup d'état is planned; prime minister stays in South Africa
Official representatives of the military in Lesotho denied planning a coup to overthrow the government and said they were cracking down on police officers accused of arming political fanatics.
Prime Minister Thomas Thabane fleded the country and announced that the country's army had surrounded his official residence and occupied government buildings in the capital, Maseru.
The prime minister brought his family to neighbouring South Africa after reporting threats of assassination.
Military spokesman Major Ntlele Ntoi said that there is de facto no coup d’état, but that the military is responding to the threat posed by “political fanatics” whose armament was imminent by the police.
"What happened this morning was that Lesotho's staff acted after receiving multiple intelligence reports that some elements were acting within the police that actually wanted to arm some of the political, partisan young fanatics who were on the verge of wrocking devastating damage," he told Voice of America.
South African government spokesman Clayson Monyela said the military action had given the impression of an overthrow.
"Even though no one has claimed to have ripped on the government through violence, all reports agree that the activities of Lesotho's defence forces have so far shown signs of a coup," he said.
Spokesmen for Lesotho's military said on Sunday that the soldiers were retreating into their barracks and the situation in the capital was calm.
In the meantime, Vice Premier Mothetjoya Metsing will take over the governance during Thabane's absence.
Thabane shared that he believes he has become a target because of his attempt to fight corruption in the country.
There have been huge tensions in Lesotho since June after Thabane suspended parliamentary sessions due to clashes in his unity government.
He said his actions had not undermined the government, in definity of denominated accusations.
Production in the eurozone at a 13-month low
The increase in production in the eurozone slowed in August to the lowest point of the past 13 months, according to a closely observed study.
The definitive Markit Purchasing Managers' Index of Manufacturing (PMI) fell to 50.7 in in August, down from 51.8 in July.
An indication above 50 indicates an expansion.
New orders declined and factories experienced slumps at a time of rising tensions between the EU and Russia over Ukraine.
The figures arrive ahead of Thursday's European Central Bank (ECB) meeting.
Markets are expecting a clear plan from the bank to deal with a hesitant eurozone recovery as well as for the looming deflation at an inflation rate of just 0.3%.
There is speculation that the ECB's header, Mario Draghi, could provide further information later this week on whether he is considering quantitative relief for the eurozone, according to those made by Britain and the US during the financial crisis.
"Although small growth is better than no growth at all, the curbing impact of increasing economic and geopolitical uncertainties has become clearer for manufacturers," said Rob Dobson, senior economist at Markit.
Factory PMI for Germany, Russia's largest trading partner in the EU, fell to its lowest level in 11 months at 51.4.
In the meantime, in the bloc's second-largest economic nation, France, the PMI dropped to 46.9.
France remains a worry child, as is Italy's relegation from a solid expansion to stagnation.
Evidence that growth incentives are no longer taking effect in the key industry mechanism Germany, as well as in Spain and the Netherlands, is also not reassuring," Mr Dobson said.
The decline in the industry presumably stokes the fire of analysts expecting further monetary or fiscal incentives.
Positive news came from the Republic of Ireland, where the PMI rose to 57.3, the highest level since the end of 1999.
Howard Archer, chief economics expert at IHS Global Insight, said: "The best you can say for the purchasing managers' index of the manufacturing sector in the eurozone in August is that the sector will continue to grow afterwards."
He added: "Producers in the eurozone are obviously finding life quite difficult at the moment, as the current heightened geopolitical tensions - especially with regard to Russia and Ukraine - magnificence uncertainties in many countries.
These increased uncertainties have quite clearly affected the economy - in particular, and consumer confidence, and presumably causes some orders to be delayed or even cancelled, in particular large-scale orders.
He said it was "becoming increasingly likely" that the ECB would eventually have to usher in some kind of QE "although we suspect this will be limited."
The hell decade of the survivors of Beslan: Ten years have passed since the terrible siege of the school, as the children entangled in it still suffer today.
Ten years ago, over 1,000 people were taken hostage by Chechen militias at a school in Beslan in southern Russia.
More than 330 people, more than half of them children, were killed in the three-day martyrdom that shocked the world.
We have returned to Beslan to find some of the victims who had escaped death in the classroom atrocity.
Exactly a decade after the horrific siege of Beslan's school, in which 334 people perished, including 186 children, the heromic survivors warned of a new apocalypse in Ukraine last night.
When fanatical Islamist terrorists captured and killed children and parents on September 1, 2004, the beginning of a new school year, that seemed like the most evil of evil.
The tragedy united West and East into disgustence, then surprisingly turned into action and hope for the future in the midst of worst misery.
Ten years later we returned to Beslan to find some of the victims who had escaped death in the classroom atrocity.
We found surprising young people who overcame resistance, though the memory of this terrorist hell will haunt them forever.
Their greatest hope?
That the war that now haunts Ukraine - and in which children like them die - is now coming to an end.
The giar who was photographed while trying to climb back into the shot school sports hall
She was photographed and famous by Russian star photographer Dimitry Beliakov when she tried only in her underwear to climb back to the smashed school sports hall after a mine exploded.
Completely confusioned, Aida searched for her mother Larissa, now 40 years old.
They were both mistaken for dead, but they survived.
"A woman told me to run for my life, but I couldn't," Aida said at the time.
My legs were covered in blood.
I stood up and climbed back in to look for my mother.
A soldier took her to safety.
After a series of surgeries, she considered herself completely healed but said yesterday: "Three months ago the pain returned.
I'm afraid I'll need surgery again.
Some shrapnels are still in my knee.
Her goal now is to become a dentist to help people as she has been helped by doctors.
"This tragedy changed my life, but it didn't break me," she says in a combative manner.
“That’s what happened to me, and you can’t change that.
Once a year, I always go to the sports hall to remember those who stayed there.
My friends and I are trying not to talk about it on other days.
Pain's too great.
My best friend and future classmate Dzera Gapoeva was killed there.
We played together when we were little and dreamed of going to school together.
“I don’t want to get married until I finish my studies and start my career.
I'm thinking of a family later.
She says the siege will always remain a part of it, although the memory becomes a bit weaker every year.
I'm glad that many people around the world still remember our horrors and we're so grateful for the help we've received from people in Britain and everywhere.
When I see the photo of myself climbing back to school online, I think many people see it for the first time, understanding our disaster and helping something like don't happen again.
The boy who fleded through hail of bullets in the opinion that his mother was dead
It was his first day of school (Russians go to school at 7 years old) and survived the three-day siege in the arms of his mother Tamara.
As a seven-year-old, he said: "Mom told me to lie down if there was an explosion, and hold her hand."
After an explosion, he thought she'd die.
She said to him, "Run."
With fear that she could be dead, he fled and saw a weeping toddler on the way, seizing it by the hand and fleeing through a hail of bullets.
His father Vladimir, who locked his son in his arms outside, said: "Damir told me that his mother had died.
He said, "I couldn't save her."
In reality, Tamara had stumbled outside and with leg injuries in the hospital, in turn assuming her son had died.
After her reunion, she said: "I was crying with joy.
I couldn't believe it.
He ran in and hugged me.
Damir was later flowned to London (by the now-disbanded News of the World newspaper) to receive the title of Barnardo's Children's Champion from then-premier Tony Blair and his wife Cherie.
"This horror haunts you every day, but I wish I could stop remembering," he said yesterday.
But I can say it's getting paler in my memory.
I remember throwing away all my toy guns right afterwards.
But now I can play computer games with shooting scenes and that's not a problem for me.
“I’m not afraid to go back to the sports hall, but I don’t think about myself.
I remember those kids I played with in the yard, and who didn't make it outside.
We'll never say they've been killed or dead.
We say they stayed in the sports hall.
I remember very well my trip to London, especially the toy store, where I could choose everything I wanted, and the ride in an open car.
But now he's afraid for place like Ukraine, where wars cause the kind of misfortune he and his friends suffered.
This horror haunts you every day, but I wish I could stop reminiscing.
"I'm so sorry for all those who suffer from the abominations," said Damir, who worked as a volunteer at a monastery last week.
I'd love to help.
I want to go to the police one day and I hope they'll take me at the training academy next year.
His mother Tamara, 48, said: "37 children from our neighborhood have been killed, can you imagine?
I remember the terrible silence after the siege, there were simply no kids who could call and walk around, and this silence lasted many months.
She vividly remembers her trip to London with Damir.
The people of London were crying as I told our story.
At that time I understood how people on the other side of the world can understand our feelings and show their support.
She was grateful to Cherie Blair and her foundation for the prize and the opportunity to travel to London in autumn 2004.
I wish I could call Cherie and just tell her herself that 10 years have passed, but I still remind myself of the meeting for her and her husband in London, and how that helped Damir and me.
I still remember how Damir wanted to eat borscht and they called all the local Russian restaurants.
I remember him playing with waiters in a restaurant. Damir shot at her with his water pistol and they ran away and played with him.
And that's been just a month after the horror.
I was surprised that the British so willingly shared our pain and supported us.
She said: "I always think this world hasn't gotten better in those 10 years.
In Ukraine, the war continues now, and people are dying every day, and I believe that many children have been killed and are still being killed.
These are just such children as ours.
I think that for many, the war is a part of the news they hear when they're bored or busy with something else.
I just know what these people are feeling, all the horror.
And I can't stop feeling that.
Nothing has changed, people still want to kill each other and that's so sad.
Damir grew up and is a very gentle and quiet boy, he'sn't aggressive or angry at all.
For example, he does not seek revenge, he is not overwhelmed by the past abominations.
I know he doesn't like to remember it, but he has six close friends out of the class and they occasionally go to the sports hall and light candles there.
When he was smaller, he suffered from it - I remember him sometimes laying down on the sofa, turning his face away from us for hours.
Not sleeping, his eyes were open.
I also still remember throwing away all his weapons and other military toys. Damir is now 17, bigger than me, handsome, intelligent and healthy.
I can't believe I was so sure for a few hours that he was dead and I'd never see him again.
When I recovered after the blast and got on my feet, I lookeed around, and saw the hell around me, with body parts lying all over.
I was absolutely sure that my little boy couldn't possibly have survived in this hell.
"I remember shouting at him, "Damir, run, run away," but I was also so sure he hadn't heard me.
But he's heard it and he's run away.
And so life, unlike that of many others, continues for us.
Georgij Ilyin, 17
The boy whose terrifying image after the siege became the symbol of the abominations of Beslan
Consolation: Beslan survivor Georgij Lyin with his mother Fatima after the siege in 20004
His blooded face was marked by fear as he ran around his life while Chechen terrorists shot down his classmates.
His image was one of the most shocking symbols of Beslan's barbarism.
His mother Fatima, 54, a female doctor, had dropped him off for his first day of school before rushing off to take her older son Wladimir to university.
"I left the yard for about two minutes before the terrorists stormed him, and so my little boy stayed there alone for three terrifying days," she recalls.
Not alone, there was a whole family of relatives of ours, but they were all killed.
Only my Georgij managed to survive.
The closer this date draws, the harder it is to think about it and talk about it.
There is not a single day when we don’t remember this tragedy.
We don't think it's been long, I mean it would have happened yesterday, some scenes from those days always haunting me.
No one's ever going to forget, I promise.
Haunting: The image of Georgij (left) was one of the most shocking of the attack and was immortalized as a statue (right).
I remember Georgij wanting to go to school that day so gladly, saying he wanted to hug his teacher.
I didn't know he was alive until I saw him on TV.
And now there's even this statue of the weeping Georgij in San Marino.
Georgij doesn't like to see the picture, and it's hidden in our home on the bookshelf.
I understand that, but I also mean that people all over the world feel our pain thanks to this picture.
