4 tips for better underwater photos and videos
If you would like to take photos or videos underwater, there is a selection of devices.
The cheapest option is a waterproof point-and-shoot, like the Nikon Coolpix AW120 or an action cam like the GoPro Hero3+ Silver Edition, both available for about £300.
I took these photos at a family party with multiple cameras, all available at $350.
Regardless of the equipment, there are a few rules for the best results.
Check your equipment twice.
Even if you have a waterproof camera, make sure that the battery compartment and other compartments are tightly locked.
Also set your camera to the photos or videos that you want to capture.
Some cameras and camcorders have scene and shooting modes to optimise the exposure time for dusky underwater conditions.
And before jumping in, check to what depth your equipment is functional.
Some cameras are only approved for up to 5 feet, others up to 50 or 60 feet.
Look for digital cameras for conventional and waterproof models in our shopping guide guide and reviews.
Take multiple shots - because many won't work.
Point-and-shoot cameras have an LCD that allows you to compose images, action cams don’t usually.
Even if you have an LCD, it’s difficult to look at this underwater, and the right image setting is a matter of luck.
So you take a lot of shots.
If your camera has a row mode that takes a series of shots with slightly changed exposure settings, use it.
Stay close to the surface.
The light gets dramatically worse the further you move away from the surface.
Therefore, if possible, stay close to the surface when taking shots in a pool, lake or the sea.
As a result, you can also add more color to your photos; the deeper you go, the less color you will see.
Go close to the objects.
This is also good advice on land, but it is all the more important underwater because of the low light conditions.
It’s especially important if you’re working with an action cam: these devices often have a fixed wide-angle lens, which means you’ll need to get closer to your objects when you’re supposed to fill the entire picture frame.
Andrew Lawson was a man of the kind whose character changes things, even in such a gigantic organization like the NHS.
As an advisory anesthesiologist, he devoted his career to the task of sparing the sick both the agony of the disease as well as the agony of treatment.
Among his patients, his wife recalls, was an MI6 employee who had to live with the devastating effects of torture.
Lawson knew that the diagnoses and diseases are important to doctors, while the patients are overwhelmingly preoccupied with something completely different: pain.
One day in 2007, however, he was the one whose suffering began.
"I didn't feel," he wrote in May this year.
I've struggled to pick myself up.
While struggling with the flu-like symptoms, he noticed he impatiently blamed his wife Juliet.
"I'd rather have everything right away than later," he noted.
When Juliet was on a business trip for a week, Lawson felt quite unusually and troubled for no reason.
There's something in the air.
He asked a colleague to do an X-ray examination of his chest.
Only two weeks agove, he had been for skiing in the French Alps.
The result of the X-ray examination was available.
He suffered mesothelioma, an incurable cancer that attacks the chestrop, i.e. the skin passing the lungs.
As with most cancers, the exact trigger is usually difficult to determine.
For example, while some smokers develop lung cancer, not all lung cancer sufferers used to be smokers.
Mesothelioma is different.
In almost all cases, the trigger is an asbestos load - contact with the fibrous building material, which was once considered "miraculous substance" but has now been recognized as fatal.
For most of us, mesothelioma is a disease that as can easily ignore.
Asbestos, after all, is a product of the past.
The most dangerous type of asbestos hasn't been used in the UK since the 1960s, when the industry voluntarily pledged to stop using it.
Even where it was still deployed, only people in certain industries worked directly with the fabric - plumbers, construction workers, carpenters and shipyard workers, for example.
As an industrial hazardous material from another time, it hardly seems worrying today.
But that complacency is inappropriate.
Britain, it seems, is witnessing the culmination of a mesolthelioma epidemic today.
There are more deaths from mesothelioma here than in any other country in the world.
With a total of 2,500 deaths per year, twice as many people die from this disease than in traffic accidents.
Annual numbers of mesothelioma deaths since 1980 and anticipated future deaths in the UK
The reason why we are experiencing the deadly effects now, even though asbestos has been illegal for years (all asbestos types were banned in 1999) is that a mesothelioma develops only after decades.
And mesothelioma's scourge isn't confined to former construction workers.
Asbestos was, and often is still, in the homes we live in, the offices we work in, the schools where we learn and the shops where we shop.
And that's why mesothelioma doesn't know class barriers, financial limits, professions, or ages.
The privileged bastions, from the elegant department stores in London to private schools, are not a shelter.
The parliament buildings are asbestos-infected.
Even in the hospitals, where we are supposed to become healthy, have already been identified as the source of this deadly carcinogen.
Andrew Lawson wasn't old.
He wasn't a plumber either.
In fact, he found it difficult to imagine where he might have come into contact with asbestos.
Then he found out.
"It looks like there was a lot of asbestos in the tunnels at Guy's Hospital, where I spent six years completing my training," he wrote.
All - students, nurses, doctors and gatekeepers - use the tunnels.
One wonders how many my peers will suffer from the same disease?
Unfortunately, he was able to answer this question at least partially.
"Of the four doctors trained at Guy's Hospital and those suffering from mesothelioma for the past five years," he wrote in a letter in 2010, "I am the last one who is still alive."
How many of us are going to get sick?
Andrew Lawson was diagnosed with mesothelioma at the age of 48.
When he died on February 17 of this year, he was 55.
This was an unusually long period of survival.
Fifty percent of all mesotheliomeric sufferers die within 8 months of diagnosis.
The disease always ends deadly.
We can therefore only repeat Lawson's question: "How many of us will still fall ill?"
Britain's leading expert on mesothelioma, Professor Julian Peto, assumes that between 1870 and 2050, if the asbestep epidemic is expected to come to a standstill in Britain, a total of 90,000 will have fallen victim to the disease.
Most of them don't have any idea at the moment that they're going to die like that.
An asbestos mine in Quebec, Canada Alamy
A quick look at the files of the courts, to which those affected often turn because of claims for damages, shows how far the scourge of mesothelioma is already widespread.
In June of this year, for example, Marks & Spencer admitted negligently exposing Janice Allen asbestos.
She worked for the chain for nine years, from 1978 to 1987 and was in charge of the clothing department at two locations - one of them the flagship store on Oxford Street.
Mrs. Allen was only 18 years old when she started working at M&S.
Now she'll have two children in her twenties.
"Before this thing," she says, "I'd never heard of mesothelioma, I didn't even really know anything about asbestos.
I never thought this would affect me.
There are few people who know a lot about asbestos.
The fact is that the term asbestos does not refer to a substance, but a group of six minerals.
The name comes from the word "asbestiform", a description of the fiber structure that makes the fabric resistant and flexible.
Three of the six were used in the construction industry.
Chrysotil, also known as white asbestos, is by far the most commonly found asbestos in buildings today.
It has been used for roof panels, floor panels, pipe insulations, boiler insulations, even for brake pads in cars.
It is less deadly than other forms of asbestos, but is nonetheless seen by the EU and the WHO as a "major health risk" with a possible death.
However, the brown asbestos (amosite) and blue asbestos (crocydolite).
Britain was once the world's largest importer of brown asbestos, and experts suspect that "there is clear but indirect evidence that this was a main reason for the uniquely high mesothelioma rates."
Marks & Spencer's employee has been exposed to asbestos at the flagship store on Oxford Street, London
Janice Allen may not have seen herself as a typical mesothelioma victim, but Julian Peto's work suggests that her story is by no means uncommon.
He has presented a study of those affected that "a substantial proportion of mesotheliomas without known exposure by work, or in-home presumably caused by asbestos contact in the environment."
According to his statement, much of these contacts are based on the "normal use and aging" of our buildings.
Looks like no one can expect to be safe.
A report from Goddard Consulting, which has examined the Palace of Westminster, shows how people, even in the heart of the government, might have been unknowingly exposed to the fabric.
In 2009, Goddard reported that the service shafts and piping behind Parliament's committee rooms were contaminated with asbestos, whose deadly fibres could be swirled by something as harmless as "strong air movements."
Members of parliament are frequently accused of caring about their own interests, but here it looks like the opposite is true.
While the Directorate of the Parliamentary Labour Service insisted that the Westminster Palace had received a "clean health certificate," it is now undisputed that £1 billion of work must be raised over several years to renovate parliament, modernize electrical installations and remove the asbestos, and that after the 2015 general elections, MPs arguably are more likely to meet at the nearby QE2 conference centre.
The Goddard report also pointed out that "the presence of asbestos has not been handled in accordance with respective provisions."
No one knows if this mismanagement is going to cost lives.
All you can do now is wait.
A person who could never accurately determine contact with asbestos is Graham Abbott, a general practitioner.
Like Andrew Lawson, 50-year-old Abbott suspects he was exposed to asbestos while working in hospitals.
"I've worked in a hospital where asbestos lawsuits have been positively decided," he says, "but I can't prove it in my case.
It's so difficult to remember all the places you've worked and the dates.
Exactly what he remembers is the day at the beginning of December 2009, on which he got such a kind of fever.
He was 45, and performed an operation just late in the evening.
All of a sudden, I had chills.
It came all of a sudden.
I've been awful.
I didn't think he could make the drive home anymore.
As a doctor, Abbott knew that the pain stemmed from the cerestial skin, the skin around his lungs.
But like Janice Allen, he had no reason to suspect a mesothelioma.
He didn't go to work for a month
Surprised doctors took X-rays of his breast and use pleuracathethers to remove fluid from the lungs and test them in the laboratory.
However, his complaints remained without a diagnosis.
His health slowly improved, and he returned to his workplace.
From time to time, however, the symptoms returned, often after exercise and heavy breathing.
In 201, a patient Abbotts with similar symptoms arrived at his practice and was later diagnosed with mesothelioma.
But even now Abbott didn't associate this with his own case.
After all, his patient was a few decades older and had worked directly with asbestos.
The connection in the case was clear.
In September 2011, Abbott's condition deteriorated again and his GP brought his CT scans and X-ray shots to a group of experts.
In December 2011, exactly two years after Abbott felt sick for the first time, a probe equipped with a camera was inserted into the cave between the ribbed and chest fur.
I'm an optimist.
I'll mostly just keep going," he says.
Honestly, I wasn’t so worried.
But Rachel, my wife, was worried.
The result of the biopsy occurred in the week between Christmas and New Year: "I was told that it was mesothelioma."
Graham Abbott: Diagnosis of mesothelioma
Suddenly Abbott had appointments with Macmillan nurses, one of which advised him to take a lawyer.
It was only now that he grasped the extent of the epidemic.
"It turned out that asbestos was very widespread, especially in large public buildings where pipes were often insulated with asbestos," he says.
The people exposed to asbestos in these buildings are now falling ill.
And so mesothelioma now meets younger people who have not worked in the typical professions.
The most dangerous pipes with asbestos insulation in hospitals were underground, so patients were presumably not exposed to the substance.
But many employees who moved from one building to another in pedestrian tunnels (like Andrew Lawson) or ate in underground canteens (as Graham Abbott often did) most likely came into contact with the toxic substances.
It shows that over several decades after the war, hospitals may have been life-saving for patients, but life-threatening for the attending physicians.
He's still being removed today.
Pupils parked their Bunsen burners on asbestos mats.
And it doesn't just affect hospitals.
Asbestos has been widely used in offices, shops, libraries and town halls due to its fabulous insulating and flame-retardant properties.
schools as well.
In fact, many people probably had the first asbestos contact in the classroom.
All over the country, in thousands of chemistry hours, pupils have parked their Bunsen burners on asbestos mats.
On websites, the topic of asbestos in schools is now frequently addressed.
And meanwhile, such innocent items in our homes, such as floor slabs or scaled roofs, have contained asbestos by default.
"It's an industrial poison that's contained in large parts of our properties," notes Andrew Morgan, the lawyer who represented Andrew Lawson in his trial against Guy's Hospital.
In one case, the only contact that occurred to an affected woman was the demolition of a garden shed in the 1970s.
So be careful how you rip off your garden shed.
The effects of the diagnosis, and knowing that the disease is incurable, is immense.
"It's taking a while to process this," says Graham Abbott.
I went back to work and tried to keep going, but I realised I couldn't focus on what I was doing.
I've been in practice for two weeks.
Then I knew that I had to go and take care of myself.
Well, I won't have another Christmas.
With the greatest difficulty was the change of perspective from a doctor's position to that of a patient.
Like innumerable patients before him, he remembers how overwhelmed he was by all the information he had to cope with.
"It's been very hard to record all of this," he says.
"I asked my doctor "how long have I got?"
And I've been given about 12 months.
I remember thinking, "Well, I won't have another Christmas.
That’s it.”
Mesothelioma is particularly insidious because it's our life support mechanism - breathing itself - that causes the deadly cancer.
Most cases of mesothelioma are caused by asbestos contact.
Asbestos consists of the smallest fibers.
When the asbestos is swirled and the fibers are inhaled, they can settle into the thoracoa, the skin around the lungs.
Asbestos fibers irritate the pectoral fur and can trigger cell mutations.
"The problem arises with the inhaled needle-shaped asbestos fibres," according to Professor Tom Treasure, a heart-thorace doctor who moved to Guy's Hospital in 2001.
Exactly the hospital Andrew Lawson suspects has exposed it to asbestos is now, ironically, a leading center in the treatment of mesothelioma.
Treasure knew Lawson and treated several others who were presumably also exposed to asbestos during their training at the hospital.
Once the asbestos needles reach the lung tissue, Treasure says, "they are pushed to the brim by the breathing process where the skin is.
From the very beginning it is invasive.
The usual options for other cancers don't work as well with mesothelioma.
The effectiveness of surgery, for example, is hotly controversial.
Some think it's worth a try.
Treasure denies this.
"You can't cut out the pectoral fur," he says.
They can't get around with the knife.
In the meantime, the cancer "doesn't respond very well on chemotherapy," the "effect shows," but doesn't heal.
"Sometimes there are long-term survivors," Treasure says.
But in the end, they all die.
Luckily, some patients actually live long, much longer than expected.
Writer Stephen Jay Gould died 20 years after diagnosis.
Two-and-a-half years after his own diagnosis, Graham Abbott is still struggling.
After contacting mesothelioma, Abbott came into contact with Andrew Lawson, who had become a one-man support and counselling office for those affected four years after his diagnosis.
"Hello, cancer center," he cheerfully imagined when making calls.
"He's been very positive," Abbott says.
He had received his diagnosis 4 years earlier and was still very active.
Originally, Abbott had been offered six chemotherapy cycles that last four months and were expected to extend his life by only one month.
"I was desperate," he says.
I wanted to give up.
Lawson, however, managed "to make things appear in a slightly more positive light."
After advising several physicians, Abbott decided to continue his treatment with Prof. Loic Lang-Lazdunski, heart thorax specialist at Guy's.
"We had an advantage because I didn't have to be expelled, I just called and got an appointment," Graham admits.
A normal patient would have to receive a referral and have to wait for approval of treatment.
Money is crucial for mesothelioma sufferers in order to perceive the best available therapies.
But if these treatments eventually inevitably fail, many sufferers stand by another financial concern - about the future of the families they leave behind.
And so they turn to the courts in the hope of compensation.
Andrew Lawson contacted Andrew Morgan of Field Fisher Waterhouse LLP.
"It's known since 1898 that asbestos is extremely dangerous to health," says Morgan.
But what changed in the 1960s is that it was recognized that even a very low burden was a health risk.
And that's where the negligence of the companies began to play a role.
Andrew Lawson and Guy's Hospital eventually settled their case, but it wasn't what Morgan calls "full-fledged compensation," as Lawson couldn't prove with absolute certainty that his mesothelioma was due to an asbestos contact at Guy's.
However, following the investigation into his death, a Guy's spokesman confirmed that "the asbestos fibres were removed in the affected basement area in the 1990s."
This was too late for Andrew Lawson.
How asbestos can be removed
In fact, for mesothelioma sufferers who strive for compensation, it is a huge problem of assigning the deadly asbestos contact to a company or a workplace - usually decades after the incidents.
Many of their former employers have been sold or closed.
Insurance documents can be lost.
And those who defend themselves against claims know that the time is for them, but certainly not with the plaintiffs.
In response, there have been significant new legislation this year that makes it easier for mesothelioma sufferers to sue for compensation, even if their former employers are no longer found.
The law has set up a £350 million fund funded by the insurance industry for all those who were diagnosed after July 2012, can prove contact but cannot sue anyone.
In those cases, plaintiffs receive 80 per cent of what a court could have awarded them in a normal compensation process - about £120,000.
About 300 successful applications to the programme are expected each year.
Andrew Morgan, like so many mesothelioma sufferers, think that £350 million represented a "very good job" of the insurance industry.
"That's a deal from insurers for insurers," he says, hintings that this sum is a quarter of what insurers would have had to pay if time hadn't gone over it and mesothelioma sufferers could find the companies and sue them normally.
Mike Penning, the then secretary of employment and pensions, also admitted that the law was "not perfect."
But both Penning and Morgan admitted that swift action was needed on a daily basis in the face of seven fatalities.
"People are suffering so much and need help today," Penning said during last year's mesothelioma law's second hearing.
At that time Graham Abbott had been in the hands of Prof Loic Lang-Lazdunski for 19 months.
After her initial deliberations, Lang-Lazdunski recommended an operation from which he expects a positive effect, unlike Tom Treasure.
This has been accompanied by radiotherapy and chemotherapy - a triple therapy that allows Lang-Lazdunski to present a survival rate of up to 40 percent over five years.
Abbott felt strengthened.
"That's, of course, with the most important thing," Abbott says.
This can always be seen in patients.
There's something that'll keep you going.
If you give up, you're getting worse.
Graham Abbott underwent surgery in March 2012.
At the end of August, he had the last of the six chemotherapy cycles.
Aftercare scans showed no signs of the disease.
Then I had a scan in March 2014.
There were several crab patches around my chest.
I just turned 50.
That's not life-threatening.
It's life-ending.
Abbott underwent six chemotherapy cycles again.
Now there is no trace of tumors visible anymore.
But the process is physically and psychologically very exhausting.
You have to think about practical things - about the money when I'm no longer, for example, or showing my wife how the boiler time setting works.
When you receive bad news, you become negative.
You have to look ahead.
As the father of Ellie, 16, and Tamsin, 14, that's not always easy.
"It's hard for parents," he says.
It's hard to know what to say and how much.
When I received my first diagnosis, I told my girls that I had a disease that I wouldn't get old with.
They reacted very differently.
Tamsin is very sociable and impetuous.
She told her friends and we soon got a lot of calls.
Ellie was more reserved.
She didn't say much.
Such conversations are something all cancer patients have to go through.
But for mesotheliomer sufferers, such conversations aren't interspersed with hope, not a trail of hope for survival.
The disease (just as in the end for Stephen Jay Gould) brings a grim security.
