China denies Hong Kong right to government election, protesters swear retaliation
On Sunday, China's parliament ruled against nominating candidates for the 2017 electorate's elections despite increased calls for democratic reforms.
This crackdown presumably fuels the long-announced protests in Hong Kong's business district, while activists initiated the planning and mobilisation within a few hours of the announcement.
The decision of the Chinese People's Congress basically allows communist leaders to dismissue all non-Beijing-faithful candidates.
"That doesn't come unexpectedly, but it makes us angry after all," said legal expert Emily Lau, chairwoman of the Democratic Party.
This isn't what Beijing promised.
They lied to Hong Kong residents.
And it's clear that we're dealing with an authoritarian regime.
Speaking to the Chinese decision, Li Fei, deputy secretary-general of the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress, said that allowing public nominations in elections would be too "chaotic."
Since 1997, when Britain returned control over Hong Kong to the Chinese, Beijing has pledged to allow residents to elect the presidency from 2017.
The Chinese leadership presented Sunday's decision as a democratic breakthrough because it gives Hong Kong citizens a direct right to vote, but the decision also makes it clear that the Chinese leadership is keeping the process firmly in its hand through a Beijing-controlled nominating committee.
According to a new clause, only candidates are to be admitted "with love of the country and love of Hong Kong".
The decision follows a summer in which some of Hong Kong's largest and most audience-effective protests have taken place for several years.
Behind Hong Kong's pro-democracy campaign is the Occupy Central With Love and Peace movement, whose organizers have threatened to foreclose the financial district if Beijing doesn't allow genuine universal suffrage.
On Sunday night, hundreds of Occupy-Central supporters had gathered in the rain outside Hong Kong's government headquarters within hours of the announcement.
At the demonstration, organisers said their movement had reached a new level of civil disobedience and they were planning waves of protests in the coming weeks.
However, they did not provide further details on this, apparently to avoid problems with the authorities.
In an online notice, the organizers announced that the movement "considered the occupation of the central district only as a last resort, an action that is only supposed to take place when all chances of dialogue are exhausted and there is no other choice.
We are sorry to have to say that all dialogue opportunities have been exhausted today and the occupation of the central district will definitely take place.
Authorities in Hong Kong have been preparing for days for Beijing's announcing, and tightened security measures, including police forces and barricades, applied on Sunday in front of the government building.
The unrest is intensified by the feeling of many in Hong Kong that they are slowly losing control of their city.
The influx of people from the maintains competition for products and services.
There is also a growing fear that Hong Kong's values such as democracy and freedom of speech are beginning to crumble under growing pressure from Beijing.
Some criticized the Occupy-Central movement's demonstrations as it jeopardizes business operations - Hong Kong's life arte.
"The protests they're talking about could cause great economic damage, depending on how many are involved in it and how long they last," said legal expert Regine Ip, who has long criticized the movement.
We don't want to stoke any fears that Hong Kong is spiraling out of control.
This impression is bad for investment.
China's state-controlled media also recently reported on Hong Kong's democracy activists as subversive operatives controlled by Western powers.
This summer, activists organized an unofficial referendum on electoral rights involving 780,000 people - more than a-fifth of Hong Kong voters.
In July, ten-thousands took part in one of the largest democracy demonstrations in the region's history.
The tradition of graduating balls in the U.S. is now transferred to the universities, where freshmen appearances by private plane are offered.
We look forward to responding to this demand and offering the UK's first luxury travel service for today's students.
In order to achieve a particularly impressive appearance at the university, the company also offers transport options such as private aircraft, Rolls-Royce Phantom, Aston Marten or McLaren P1.
