Feminists criticize racial issue and police behaviour after Ferguson
The unarmed teenager was killed earlier this month by a police officer in Ferguson, Mo.
The attendees raised their hands in the air and sang, "Hands up, don't shoot," while they waited before the funeral.
After two-week protests in Ferguson, Mo, over the shooting of unarmed teenager Michael Brown, blogger Miriam Zoila Perez noticed a change in the online discussions of white female feminists.
According to her experience, white feminist commentators put sex in front of race when it comes to appealing to reproductive rights and income inequalities.
However, while tensions in the Midwest increased and received attention across the country, Perez saw that the responses of white women revolved 100% around the breed.
Compared to the responses of black women shortly after the August 9 shots, the personal essays with titles such as "Thought on Ferguson by a white woman" and "Feminism isn't just about the oppression of women" came relatively late.
But for Perez, that meant an important change.
"I don't think you're finding a lot of people in feminism who being about the white and having privileges in such a high-class way," she said.
People felt urged to express something about the incidents.
It's characteristic of the crisis we're in and because the situation in Ferguson is so ethnized they have to express that.
For English professor Koritha Mitchell from Ohio State University, Ferguson uncovered problems faced by black Americans every day but who aren't considered "women's issues" in cultural space.
"I can post something funny about myself and my partner and get 150 likes from everywhere," Mitchell said.
When I post something about how people of color are under siege in their own land, silence is deafening.
"How can it be safe for women to talk publicly about a relationship, but not about security in public spaces?" she asked.
For black women like Mitchell, who have studied race, gender, and sexuality in U.S. history, there is no dichotomy between racial and gender problems.
For them, black women haven't had the luxury of neatly separating the themes; they live every day in the combined reality.
While white women now connect the issues of race and gender in mainstream feminist circles, Angela Hattery, professor of women and gender studies at George Mason University, says her predecessors did the opposite.
"Between 1865 and 1890, at least 10,000 black men were lynched and the justification was almost always the rape of a white woman," Hattery said.
White women had to be involved in the story to justify lynching.
For Hattery, the split between white and black women at the point where white female fighters for women's suffrage like Susan B. Anthony analyzed the country in the late years of the 19th-century and found that the struggle for the right to vote could only be successful for one group each: women or blacks.
"They made the decision to stand up for women's suffrage, and the right to vote for blacks should come later," Hattery said.
When you remember things like this, you become aware with power why women of color don’t trust white women.
We didn't do our job well.
We have never helped black women protect their husbands and sons.
Even after women were given the right to vote in 1920, it took another decade for white women to organize against lynching in the Association of Southern Woman for the Prevention of Lynching.
The group originated nearly 40 years after the original request for help from pages of black women, Mitchell says.
For Mitchell, the tide of white feminist responses to questions raised by Ferguson - police behavior, racial discrimination - is a mirror of the times in which white feminists "catches" played "catches" with the problems that black women have been grappling with for generations.
“Wouldn’t it be great if the activists who have such traditional feminist platforms take the right to raising a child as seriously as they do with the right to contract?” Mitchell asked.
Feminists of the second generation, who built their career on the struggle for reproduction rights and access to contraceptive means, are now trying to understand what that means.
Well-known white feminist activist Gloria Steinem went on Facebook two weeks after the shooting of Michael Brown and posted a pointed article by Rebecca Carroll, the Guardian's columnist, in which she calls for more protests from white Americans on the subject of racism.
"I hope that women who have a different but parallel reason to understand a danger inherent in the body - and opinions on the race that are significantly different in public polls - will cite the changes," Steinem wrote.
Although Steinem stands for a rather intersectional feminism, in which racial and gender issues are addressed, some of their comments on the racial issue in America are astounded.
Christina Sommers, a researcher at the American Enterprise Institute and author of "Who Stole Feminism?" told She The People that young men in the United States, especially young men of color, are "far more vulnerable than their sisters," but Steinem's remarks on Ferguson are contrary to her previous criticism.
There are now hundreds of special programs for girls and young women, but almost nothing for boys.
But when the White House recently launched a small program, My Brother's Keeper, to help vulnerable young black and Hispanic men, there were annoying reactions from many feminists, including Gloria Steinem.
The relationship of the police to the black community is not the only problem that pushes into the foreground of mainstream feminism.
After the U.S. border police apprehended nearly 63,000 unaccompanied minors on the country's south-western border this year, the issue of immigration reform is also stepping back to the surface - this time as a women's issue.
Andrea Mercado, co-chair of We Belong Together, an organization that mobilizes women for immigratory reform, said that for a revisitation of the issue as an essential women's issue, she only had to publish the stories of immigrant women.
"These speak for themselves," Mercado said.
When the vast majority of work visas go to male immigrants, the women who accompany them are in a position where they are susceptible to domestic violence and exploitation.
"These stories find a reverberation in women's organizations," she said.
"Pig house" in Ipswich is for sale
The image of a house for sale was removed by real estate agents when it became clear that it showed a large pig it had made itself comfortable in the living room.
The detached house has one bedroom, is located on a private street in Ipswich and is offered for £120,000.
The details included an illustration of the house's living room - which included a pig who had bedded his head on a sofa.
Real estate agent Connells said the pig in the photo was the owner's pet.
"However, the photo has been erroneously released and has since been removed," a spokeswoman for the company said.
In the house's exposé, Connells described the former meeting house as a "unique one-bedroom single-family home."
Although the broker has removed the image from the website, it still appears on promotional material.
The home sale caused interest on social media when some asked if the pig was included in the purchase price.
The SW-City of Harvey is home to a new millionaire after lottery win
It's a lottery player from Harvey this month
The search for a new millionaire in south-west Perth continues, with a lucky winner receiving nearly $1.1 million from Saturday's lottery draw.
A newspaper kiosk in Harvey drew the jackpot for the second time in five years in the draw of the first division, after £1 million in November 2009.
However, the lucky winner has yet to come forward.
Shopkeeper Steve Forward said the profit made for talk in the small town in the south-west of Perth.
The city is talking about nothing else, and everyone's pretty excited.
We thought such a profit could occur.
Eight WA winners celebrated wins in the first division last month, a due graduation for a couple of happy weeks for the players in the state.
Last week, a player from Canning Vale became one of the five millionaires in August after a couple from Belmont made the $7.5 million mega profit just a few weeks earlier.
The winning couple had been playing for twenty years and said the win allowed them the possibility of a trip around the world and buying a new house.
The millionaire from Harvey is the 59th winner of the first division in the WA this year, with a total of nearly $85 million.
Leonidas asked for the gladiatorial arena
Not only the gladiators stood ankle-deep in the mud at the first Rheinbacher Römertag in the amusement park.
But the attraction of the event was strong enough to attract well over 1000 visitors yesterday, who braved the rain showers and the autumnal cold and were completely thrilled by the historical spectacle.
There was a colorful series of depictions, performances, games, exhibits and information about the time of the Romans and their significance for the Rhineland.
The main attraction of the day was undoubtedly the martial fighter squad from the "Amor Mortis" gladiatorial school, which used every rain break to demonstrate its robust martial art in the arena groomed with soft sand.
Gladiator owner "Leonidas" proved to be a humorous and knowledgeable presenter and sent his men into the ring one after the other for a heroic duel in various gladiatorial genres.
At first, the "provocators" on the row were with a rectangular shield and short sword as well as leg splint on the left leg and arm splint on the right arm.
This was followed by the "Essedarii" with breast shield, shortsword and throwing rim as well as an iron gladiatorial helmet, leg and arm protection.
Particularly impressive was the appearance of the "Retiarius", which competed against the "Secutor" with throwing net, trident and dagger and knocked the boys to the ground after a short fight.
Then the swords crashed into each other metallically, the shields shoved and the litters flew through the arena swirling that one could become afraid and afraid for the health of the gladiators.
But the imposing "Summa Rudis", the muscular referee, always had an attentive eye.
"We wanted to revive Roman everyday life and make the culture of the Roman period tangible," explained Lorenz Euskirchen, chairman of the organizer "Freundeskreis Römerkanal", the intention behind the spectacle.
After two years of preparatory time, an "Oppidum Romanum", a Roman city for one day, became an "Oppidum Romanum", as Vice Mayor Claus Wehage noted in his greeting.
After all, the Romans not only brought war and conquest in our region, but also the Roman culture and numerous buildings that we still admire today.
Remains of this could also be admired at Roman's Day, for example "Aqueduct-Marmor" from the eleven-meter-high and 1400-meter-long Roman canal bridge, which once stood near Lüftelberg.
The Frontinus-Society contrasted the water and energy supply once and now, and Managing Director Petra Fricke knew: "We still benefit from the knowledge of that time today".
This is also confirmed graduate surveyor Ralph Heiligener of the "Engineering TeamZwei" from Rheinbach, who presented surveying instruments of the Romans such as Chorobat, Groma or Dioptra.
The Roman villa in Blankenheim presented sketches and models from the excavations in 1894 as well as bricks from the original hypokauste, the warm air heating that oncemorated the Römervilla.
Impressive is also the footprints left by wolf and fox two millennia ago in the footsteps made of clay before they were burned.
In 2011, the office for the preservation of soil monuments at the Landesverband Rheinland excavated the remains of a Roman villa in the Gewerbepark Nord in Rheinbach.
Enclosure walls of a farm building with a darre for drying grain and malting were found.
The building probably belonged to a Villa Rustica, an agricultural estate, explained the archaeologist Dr. Jennifer Morscheiser.
A number of finds from these excavations presented them in a glass showcase, including a large plate, several fine glass vessels as well as parts of a delicate necklace.
The villa probably existed from the first to the fourth century AD, to the incursion of the Germans in the then Roman Rhineland, Morscheiser suspects.
With her, the children also dealt with the Roman windmill game, similar to today's milling game, or, like the Roman disciples, they wrote their names with metal handles on wax plaques.
At the stand of the Römerthermen Zülpich, the children were allowed to lay mosaicks themselves, make glass mosaics at the stand of the Rheinbach glass museum and serve a heavy wheat mill made of stone at the stand of the Eifel Nettersheim nature centre in order to grind the flour for a flatbread or cereal.
With the faithfully recreated Roman touring car by two cold-blooders, the visitors drove through the amusement park and also passed the impressive booth of the Municipal Gymnasium, which presented the results of their "Roman Project Weeks".
With a Roman week from 13 to 21 September, the municipality of Wachtberg will also be involved in the Rhineland-wide proclaimed Roman year 2014, which is to commemorate the death of Emperor Augustus 2000 years ago.
Together with the local and historical associations as well as the Wachtberger Wander-Verein, the Partnerschaftsverein and the Laienspielkreis Oberbachem are offered, among other things, thematic hikes, a lecture, cooking evenings as well as guided tours for school classes and commemorate Wachtberg’s Roman past.
For example, a Roman legionnaire will talk about his work on the trival Wachtberg, plants will be presented as living monuments from the Roman period and a witty lesson in terms of international understanding between a group of Wachtberger "Germans" and drawn Romans.
As part of a project week, the Adendorf elementary school is dedicating itself to the Roman theme.
With the Roman Week, the numerous traces of the Romans in Wachtberg, which are often no longer visible at first glance, are to be experienced again.
In Wachtberg’s town hall, a small presentation will commemorate the discovery of the Berkum Matronic Shrine in 1879.
Ebola: The epidemic weighs on West Africa's economy
The Ebola epidemic in West Africa is also a major blow to the economy.
Markets are orphaned - and international companies distance themselves.
The devastating Ebola epidemic deals a major blow to West Africa's economy.
The harvest is rotting in the fields, the mines are deserted and the markets orphaned - the virus has severely affected the region.
Some international companies are also already distanced themselves.
"The Ebola epidemic is not only a medical crisis, but also an economic one," said Donald Kaberuka.
Liberia is the hardest affected
"It's a total disaster, we're losing a lot of money," says Alhaji Bamogo, who sells clothes at the second-largest market in Liberia's capital Monrovia.
Only those who want to buy food or something for disinfection against Ebola come to the market.
Liberia is the hardest hit by the virus, with more than 600 people falling victim to the fever.
Health authorities recently declared that the disease had penetrated into every corner of the country.
The virus has also spread in Guinea, Sierra Leone and Nigeria.
In total, more than 1500 people have already died, with thousands more infected.
Foreign Firms Withdraw Employees
Everywhere in resource-rich countries, companies are suspending their operations.
Foreign companies such as the steel company Arcelor Mittal are withdrawing their employees, several international airlines are no longer flying to the affected regions.
The local traders and farmers are also reacting: only a few merchants still leave their hometowns to get supplies.
In some areas in Sierra Leone and Liberia that are quarantined, among other things, the cocoa and coffee beans, which are important for export, are rotting in the fields.
Farmers don't dare to leave their home for the harvest.
Food is scarce
"People are going to travel less and less," says Phillipe De Vreyer, Parisian expert on West Africa's economy.
The man, for example, who usually goes to the market and sells his vegetables, prefers to stay at home.
The population is therefore feeling the consequences of the epidemic directly and urgently: food is becoming scarce.
In the markets, stocks of staples such as rice are dwindling.
At the same time, the region, which is heavily dependent on international companies, threatens imagination - with far-reaching consequences.
The biggest danger is that international companies basically avoid the area for a long time, says Philippe Hugon of the French think tank Iris.
It is important how far and how long the virus will spread.
"Investors can get the impression that it is dangerous to live in Guinea, Sierra Leone and Liberia and thus invest," warns Hugon.
The disease also strains the strained state budgets of the states
The disease also weighs on the already strained state budgets of the affected states.
Credit rating agency Moody's fears that their budgets will be severely strained by the high spending on the health care system and, in addition, by the deteriorating economy.
The African Development Bank has already pledged to help $60 million (€45 million) health systems of the affected countries.
The effects are already noticeable in Nigeria, where only a few Ebola cases have been diagnosed so far.
The oil-rich region in the southern Niger Delta has not yet been affected.
Yet elsewhere, many people are already staying at home for fear of contagion.
Reservations at hotels are also declining significantly.
Shooters celebrate their 50th anniversary
The Schützenverein "Tannenwald" Berngau celebrates its 50-year founding festival from 12 to 14 September.
Preparations for the 50-year founding festival from 12 to 14 September arre in full swing at Schützenverein Tannenwald.
A large entertainment evening with the group "Dorfrocker" is planned on Friday, September 12.
On Saturday at 6 p.m. is the big firecracker shooting.
Subsequently in the large marquee is the mood evening with the "Stoapfälzer Spitzbuam".
Brass band provides for the music
Highlights on Sunday are the festive service and 9.30 a.m. in the parish church and the great procession at 2 p.m.
In the afternoon is also the prize distribution of the federal and gau shooting.
A particularly beautiful sight will be the 25 festive ladies with their dirndls.
Festkapelle is Berngau's Blaskapelle.
Mayor Wolfgang Wild and State Secretary Albert Füracker took over the patronage.
The original designation "Schützengesellschaft Tannenwald Berngau" dates from 1907, when the association for "social entertainment was founded on 21 March by means of exercise in room spout and small-caliber shooting".
The Nazi regime had prompted the dissolution of the association in 1937.
It was not until 1964 that 19 young men found each other, who launched the "new" Schützenverein "Tannenwald" Berngau on April 30 at Gasthaus Lukas.
Karl March and Adolf Ried led the club as shooting champions through a difficult initial period, in which it was mainly about finding a club premises and installing the shooting operation.
Already in the autumn of 1965, the Schützenverein started in the inn "Leininger" with two teams in the lap competitions.
In the following years, the sports shooters, who were now organized in seven teams, established themselves in all classes up to the 1st Gauliga.
The club records tumbled annually and the magic limit of 380 rings was reached for the first time in 1980 by Rudi Meier.
Just as the performance of active shooters have increased, the number of members has also developed positively.
After the "Leininger" has no longer met the requirements of a modern Schützenverein, the then board of directors under the leadership of Johann Rinn decided to create a future domicile in the basement rooms of the new "Landgasthof Härteis".
After a working capacity of 2700 hours on May 8, 1988, Pastor Strasser granted the church blessing for the new shooting rooms.
One of the great highlights in the club's history was certainly the 44th Gauschießen, combined with the solemn consecration of the new shooting flag in July 1991.
On April 23 and 24, 1994, the Schützenverein celebrated its 30th anniversary.
In 1995, a firecracker group was founded.
Josef Schmalzl became a firecracker's consultant.
A great challenge for the "Böllerer" and for the association as a whole was the organization of the Bavarian Firecracker Meeting 2005.
In many hours of voluntary work last year, the members of the association modernized the rooms and the shooting facility in the Berngauer Hof and thus brought them to the latest technology.
Eleven teams in round competitions
You can also see the sporting achievements of the Schützenverein.
A total of eleven teams are currently taking part in the round competitions: six air rifle teams, one air gun squad team, one sports gun KK1, one youth and two AH teams.
The 1st team with Lars Ellrich, Katharina Ellrich, Steffi Grad and Kerstin Kellermann has risen from the Gauoberliga (highest league in Schützengau ANB) to the Bezirksliga 1.
The 2nd team with Verena Moosburger Alexandra Meier, Eva Schuhmann and Tobias Graml has ascended from Gauliga Nord to the Gauoberliga.
In addition to shooting sports, events such as buckling, carnival and royal ball, club excursion and Christmas party have a tradition.
The shooters are also involved in the festivals in the parish and community.
Tony Stewart suffers accident upon return to the racetrack
Tony Stewart's return to the track ended about halfway through his race at the Atlanta Motor Speedway.
Stewart raced against the wall for the second time after his right front tyre burst into lap 172 and ended his evening.
Stewart drove his damaged car into the garage and left it without talking to journalists.
His team principal Chad Johnston said his 14-strong team was disappointing but is now focused on next weekend's race in Richmond, Virginia.
"I wish we'd had a better effort and a better result for him," Johnston said.
We're going to Richmond hoping we can do better there.
Stewart was already driving into a wall earlier at the race on Sunday evening after a collision with Kyle Busch and had to have the right side of his car repaired.
"I went into it today with quite good hopes for a good result," Johnston said, adding: "It just didn't work out."
Not long after the second accident, Stewart's car was loaded into the tractor and the team had packed and was ready to depart.
Stewart dropped three NASCAR Sprint Cup races after his car hit and killed another driver at a Cinestral race in northern New York State.
He decided to return this week and win either in Atlanta or at the next race at Richmond to win a place in the chase.
He returned to his workplace during an investigation into the tragic incident leading to the death of 20-year-old Kevin Ward Jr., who had stepped onto the carriageway during a race to confront Stewart.
Authorities said on Friday that investigations into the cause of the accident will continue for at least another two weeks.
No decision has yet been made as to whether Stewart must face an indictment.
The three-time champion was received with great applause when he was introduced before the race.
After a start at 12th position, Stewart had already penetrated into the first 10 shortly afterwards and was temporarily in fourth place.
After a reboot in lap 122, Buschs
Wagon with the No. 18 out of control after the exit from turn 2 and collided with Stewart, with both carriages driving against the wall.
Steart continued, but dropped back to 21st place.
Dance-loving offspring wanted
Just try: Laura, Lena, Lisa, Marie, Bettina, Emma and director Lisa Neitzel (from left) are looking forward to competitors.
Like many other clubs, the jazz groups on TV Jahn make the all-day lessons at the secondary schools to create.
After the summer holidays, some groups decimated sharply.
Now we are increasingly looking for a sport that is healthy and great fun.
Lisa Neitzel's two jazz and modern-dance groups, formerly a dancer in the jazz and modern dance formation "Spirits," are only nine children left.
"Three in the eight- to twelve-year-old group, six in the group of Twelve to 15-year-olds," the 20-year-old recounts.
With more activists, the whole thing would be significantly more fun, "you could dance completely different choreographies," she says.
If you feel like dancing, you can sniff into the training lesson on Thursdays between 5 p.m. and 6 p.m. between 5 p.m. and 6 p.m. on Fridays between 5 p.m. and 6:30 p.m. in the dance sports room at the former Hotel Meyer, Freistraße 9.
"The training regularly starts with a warm-up program," says the trainer.
Various choreographies are practiced afterwards.
JazzDance has the feature that there is no fixed form and is therefore open to many styles.
Elements from folklore through classical to sports and acrobatics are processed in jazz dance.
Of course, conviviality is not neglected either.
"We're also going out to eat, watching dance movies and we've stayed in the dance room before," says Lisa Neitzel.
In the group you can make friendships, share a beautiful hobby and do a lot for your own motor development.
If jazz dance isn’t like that, you can also sniff into one of the other offers of the TV Jahn.
Children and adolescents can always sniff into the lessons on Wednesdays in children's dance/jazz dancing, bambini ballet, video clip dancing and hip-hop.
The dance coach is Francisco Afonso.
Adult couples are welcome to the dance evenings on Monday and Wednesday.
If you feel liked for Discofox, Slow Waltzes, Viennese Waltzes, Rumba, Cha-Cha-Cha and Tango, you can stop by and participate.
Bulgarian prison wardens protest nationwide
Hundreds of prison employees from all over Bulgaria have organized a nationwide protest in front of the Ministry of Justice in the capital Sofia.
In the peaceful demonstration, they repeated their demands for restoration of their old working conditions, which have recently been changed.
Higher salaries are also on a list of claims to be discussed with interim Justice Minister Hristo Ivanov.
Officers have been symbolically protesting the new working hours for a month and are calling for a return to the previous 24-hour shifts.
Despite the meetings between the prison staff's union and representatives of the Ministry of Justice, no agreement was reached, the national Bulgarian Ratio (BNR) reported.
Negotiations are ongoing, the director-general for the implementation of punitive measures, Rosen Zhelyazkov told the BNR.
The prison staff's protest is expected to be assisted by members of the Association of Employees of the Ministry of Interior
Ukrainian army has abandoned Luhansk airport
Pro-Russian fighters continue to advance in eastern Ukraine.
After heavy skirmishes, which allegedly also involved Russian tank battalions, the Ukrainian army today, Monday, abandoned the airport of the rebel stronghold Luhansk.
At the same time, the army announced that seven soldiers had been killed in the past 24 hours.
Ukrainian President Petro Poroshenko had previously drawn personnel consequences from the heavy defeats of his army in the fight against the separatists and announced changes in the supreme army leadership, without giving details.
Pro-Russian separatists fired plane
In the fight against Ukrainian government forces, pro-Russian separatists once again shot down a warplane and two military helicopters.
About 100 soldiers were also injured or killed in battles in the Donetsk region, the insurgents said on Monday, according to Russian agencies.
Coast Guard ships sunk
The insurgents also claimed to have sunk for the first time in the Sea of Azov for the first time of the Ukrainian coast guard.
Ukraine's border guard confirmed that two of its ships had come under fire near the port city of Mariupol on Sunday.
Seven border guards were injured, said a spokesman in Kiev to the broadcaster 112.ua.
Two crew members were believed to be missing.
Local medics blamed Russia for the incident.
The separatists also reported having brought several localities in the Donetsk area under their control.
Diplomatic Offensive in Minsk
In the Belarusian capital Minsk, the Ukraine contact group is meeting today.
In addition to representatives of Ukraine, Russia and the OSCE, an envoy of separatists from eastern Ukraine should also take part.
In advance, all actors used their appearances to publicly announce their demand.
NATO won't breach agreements with Russia
As a result of the planned deployments in Eastern Europe as a result of the Ukraine crisis, NATO will not break its agreement with Russia.
NATO Secretary-General Anders Fogh Rasmussen said in Brussels on Monday that NATO adheres to the establishment agreement of the NATO-Russia Council of 1997.
However, the NATO chief blamed Russia "of a crass breach" of the agreement.
"Today we have to face the reality that Russia does not regard NATO as a partner.
Documents and statements from the Russian side say that they regard us as opponents.
Stronger NATO Presence
Rasmussen has also announced that it intends to strengthen NATO's presence in Eastern Europe.
A "readiness action plan" to be decided at the NATO summit in Wales will ensure that "we will have more visible NATO presence in the east as long as necessary," Rasmussen said in Brussels on Monday.
Kyiv and Moscow agree on new gas talks
Russia and Ukraine have officially agreed on new talks to resolve their gas dispute.
Negotiations are scheduled for next Saturday, Russian Energy Minister Alexander Nowak said on Monday.
It is still awaited for a decision of the European Union, whether it also sends a representative for the talks.
Due to the dispute over the price of gas, Ukraine has not been getting fuel from Russia for weeks.
Most recently, EU Energy Commissioner Günther Oettinger mediated between the two sides.
Ukraine had warned that Russia could also disrupt gas supplies for Europe.
Europe draws around one-third of its natural gas from Russian sources, a large part of which flows through Ukraine.
Merker says word of power: Toll will come "quite clear"
Now the chancellor is clearly committed to it once again.
However, it is not a charter for all complicated details.
Persistent concerns from the CDU against the passenger car toll have grimmed the CSU increasingly.
German Chancellor Angela Merkel has spoken a word of power for the introduction of a car toll after persistent resistance from her CDU.
"To be very clear: she is in the coalition agreement, and she will come," the party leader said on Monday after meetings of the CDU's top bodies in Berlin.
For the time being, however, Merkel did not commit to a detailed model.
In the black-and-red government program, "certain parameters" should be mentioned.
“Federal Transport Minister Alexander Dobrindt (CSU) “made a proposal, and it is now being worked on.”
The NRW-CDU, however, insists on its no to a toll on all roads.
Merkel stressed that Finance Minister Wolfgang Schäuble (CDU) is also constructively involved in the deliberations for a bill.
In addition to the topic, he also spoke with Dobrindt about further possibilities to activate more private capital for investment in infrastructure, for example in broadband expansion.
"There's nothing special about it." Schäuble had left a "Spiegel" report uncommented on Sunday that his house was reviewing its own concept to reimburse highway investors with toll revenue - after the 2017 election.
CSU leader Horst Seehofer then warned against developing toll concepts in the cabinet against the leading minister.
CDU federal deputy Armin Laschet reiterated his objections.
"The coalition agreement never assumed that all county and federal roads would be surveyed," the North Rhine-Westphalia CDU state chairman said.
In this respect, it is an important signal that Seehofer has now announced to talk about the topic again.
Resistance against the plans also comes from the CDU state associations Baden-Württemberg and Rhineland-Palatinate.
The background is fears that a toll will affect small border traffic to neighbouring countries.
Seehofer had demanded because of the toll criticism from ranks of the CDU coalition loyalty.
The left sees the plans as a failure in view of the dispute in the Union.
"The Murk's toll is dead," party leader Bernd Riexinger told the Neue Osnabrücker Zeitung (Monday).
Dobrindt wants to introduce a vignette obligation on all German roads, which is scheduled to start in 2016.
Domestic car owners, however, may not be burdened according to the coalition agreement.
Therefore, they are supposed to receive full compensation for the toll via the motor vehicle tax.
However, according to EU law, the model must not disadvantage foreign drivers because of their nationality.
Genetic disorder often misdiagnosed
One Briton says she thought as a result of a misdiagnosis over many years that she would die.
Karin Rodgers lived almost her entire youth in the belief that she had only a few years to live after being diagnosed with another disease.
She actually suffered from Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease (CMT) - a group of inherited disorders that damages the nerves outside the brain and spinal cord.
Charity CMT UK said misdiagnoses are a common problem in people with CMT because so little is known about this disease.
About 23,000 people in the UK are believed to suffer from CMT, which can lead to motor disturbances such as muscle weakness, an unsafe gait and curved toes.
Sufferers also suffer from numbness or pain. The disease is incurable and progressive, which means the symptoms get worse over time.
When Rodger's was 13, she believed she was suffering from Friedreich ataxia (FA) - a disease with very poor chances of survival.
Rodgers thought she'd sit in a wheelchair before she turned 18 and die before her 30th birthday.
The mother of two children, who is 51 years old today, said: "As a child I knew I couldn't do the same as the others.
I kept falling and everything lasted longer with me.
I could never ride roller skates or skateboarding with the other kids and was often teased at school because of my way of walking.
Rodgers said that at the age of 13 after several surgeries to relieve her Achilles tendons and stretching her feet, she was viewing her medical record when her doctor had left the room and reased that she was suffering from FA.
"I felt guilty because I'd been curious, so I didn't tell anyone about that, but I went home, looked for the disease in the library and wrote to the FA society," she said.
When I received an answer, I was shocked and terribly panicked.
I thought I'd be in a wheelchair at 18 and dying at 25, and slowly losing all my skills in between.
I turned it all out with myself and planned my own burial.
She said that when she turned 17, she realized that her skills hadn't diminished as much as she had imagined, interviewing her doctor.
He just got up and hugged me and said, "my dear, I don't think you've got that, otherwise you'd already be in a wheelchair now."
I think you've got something far less life-threatening.
After some genetic tests, CMT was detected.
"When he explained to me what CMT was, I felt like I was a winner," she said.
Charity CMT UK has launched a CMT awareness month to draw attention to this disease.
Stabsfeldwebel a. D. Berni Diether has been working as a fireworker since the closure of the former troop training ground
For almost ten years, the former troop training ground in Münsingen has not been shot.
Nevertheless, every month deadly ammunition still appears in the heart of the Swabian Alb biosphere area.
The grenades are sometimes still so dangerous that they have to be blown up on site.
A deafening bang echoes across the heart of the biosphere during the blasts.
In December 2004, the last sharp shoot fell at the Münsingen military training ground, which was closed at the end of 2005 after 110 years of military operation.
Since that time, the 6500-hectare area has been the centrepiece of the Swabian Alb biosphere area, which is open to hikers and cyclists on 13 designated trails.
Tax burden on business people in the Washington area rises with economic recovery
Recreating the economy means more customers, more sales and new jobs.
It also means higher taxes.
Government and local tax rulings for companies in the country rose slightly last year, while the economic recovery accelerated, according to a newly published study last week, and firms in the Washington area were no exception.
Companies in the District, Maryland and Virginia areas collectively paid $27.6 billion to government and local agencies in the 2013 financial year, a 3.8 percent increase from $26.6 billion in 2012 revenue.
Companies' state and local tax burden rose 4.3 percent to $671 billion last year, compared to 3.9 percent a year earlier, and it was the third consecutive year of growth after several years of declining notices in 2009 and 2010.
State taxes increased more sharply than local levies, by 4.3 percent, according to the study carried out by the services agency Ernst & Young with the Center for State Taxation, a tax policy group.
More than half of the District's tax revenue, 56 percent, comes from corporate taxes, while 36 percent of Maryland's revenue comes from companies.
Virginia generates the lowest tax revenue from companies at 28 percent.
Much of the increase in tax revenues is due to a recovery in corporate property values, the researchers say, leading to a 3.7 percent increase in property taxes this year after the three consecutive years with growth rates of less than 1 percent.
While much of these gains came from large states such as California, New York and Texas, the process also seems to be true for the Washington area.
Companies in the District, Maryland and Virginia collectively paid over $10 billion in state and local property taxes last year, compared to $9.6 billion in 2012 - a 4.2 percent increase over the course of a year.
But the apparent recovery in property values isn't as effective in Maryland's increase in government and local tax receipts, as property taxes here amount to just over a-fifth of corporate tax rulings.
Businesses in Virginia and D.C. pay nearly half of their state and local taxes in the form of property taxes.
"In Maryland, a large portion of the real estate, especially in the D.C. tax area, is either owned by the government or non-professional organizations that don't pay property taxes," said Douglas Lindholm, managing director of the Center on State Taxation.
Maryland, for example, is forced to rely much more on income taxes in order to be able to provide the same services offered by other states.
The recent recovery in tax revenues by companies cannot be attributed solely to the recovery of the real estate market
Corporate earnings also appear to be recovering, according to data.
Companies in the region reported income taxes at state organizations of $2.3 billion compared to $2.1 billion in 2012.
Maryland took in $1 billion in corporate income taxes, the region's tallest amount.
While the trend is going in the same direction, the local tax burden for companies does not rise evenly in all three places.
The rise rate in the district, at 4.3 percent, corresponded to the national average, while the increase in Maryland was significantly higher, 4.9 percent.
Only Virginia reported below-average tax revenue of 4.1 percent.
Virginia has significantly lowest corporate income taxes at 6 percent in all three offices (there are fixed corporate tax rates in all three).
The corporate tax rate in Maryland is 8.25 per cent and in the district at a relatively high 9.975 per cent.
A comparable study by the U.S. Chamber of Commerce earlier this year showed that lower state and local corporate taxes are demanded in Virginia and a better tax climate prevails overall than in Maryland.
In the study, the district was not examined.
Despite the benefits, Virginia is under pressure by its southern neighbor.
North Carolina has passed legislation to cut its corporate tax rates from 6.9 per cent this year to 6 percent, the same rate as Virginia, and the rate will drop to 5 percent next year.
If the state continues to meet its income targets next year, that law could reduce the state's corporate income tax rate to 3 percent by 2017.
In the meantime, the small businesses in the Washington area seem to be doing better, and they are therefore paying more government and local taxes, according to the latest study.
Corporate taxes paid as personal income taxes by small business executives - a transitional structure according to which most small businesses are organized - in the District, Maryland, and Virginia rose 20 percent last year to $2.4 billion, a significantly faster growth rate than corporate taxes overall.
The researchers say the trend and increase in corporate taxes across the country is likely to continue this year, with a 6.2 per cent increase in general multi-defense tax revenue for the state and local governments in the first three quarters of 2014 compared to the same period last year.
Still, the Washington area could be lagging behind if the federal government's spending cut affects the labor market in the region.
Partner city: German beer for Guangzhou
Hops and malt - a popular combination even in China.
German beer enjoys an excellent reputation in China.
Frankfurt's partner city in China now wants a brewery and also hopes for a direct flight between the two cities.
German beer, brewed in Frankfurt's partner city Guangzhou - that's the urgent wish of the officials in China.
"We would be very happy if a German brewery were to settle in our city," South China's deputy party secretary, Li Yiwei, said this week while visiting an SPD delegation to his city.
Again and again in the course of the conversation, the functionary of the ruling Chinese Communist Party came back to this concern of the Twelve-million metropolis.
Guangzhou, which is also called canton in Germany, is one of China’s richest cities.
That’s why people have the purchasing power to be able to afford German beer.
Hessian SPD chairman Thorsten Schäfer-Gümbel, who led the German political delegation, told the hoster that he would look for a possible partner for the project in Germany.
"It doesn't take much for beer: good water, cereals and zest for life," he added at lunch with the party leader in Guangzhou.
This joie de vivre already radiates Mr. Li, he flattered the host.
Subsequently, the functionary from Guangzhou bumped with Schäfer-Gümbel - but with red wine, which was served for the food.
The Chinese brand Tsingtao goes back to German brewing.
Tsingtao beer is brewed according to the German Purity Law.
Greater business transactions
A second wish of the Chinese hosts would be a direct flight connection between Frankfurt and Guangzhou, Vice Party leader Li said.
Lufthansa had offered the flight earlier, but cancelled in 2012 for economic reasons.
"It would be worthwhile again," said the local politician.
“The commerce between our two cities continues to increase.”
Currently, travelers would have to take the detour via Changsha in Hunan province, Li complained.
Guangdong Province's administration, whose capital is Guangzhou's capital, is also hoping for the flight connection.
The deputy governor of the province, Chen Yunxian, emphasized this wish in his conversation with the Social Democrats.
"That's a very good idea," Schäfer-Gümbel replied - Chinese Vice Minister Li Jinjun, an intimate connoisseur of Germany, nodded approvingly.
Welcomed partnership
Guangzhou's people's vice-party chief hopes he will be able to visit Frankfurt for a partnership meeting next year.
Li Yiwei was already on the Main, but not yet in this function.
He only came into office this year, after his predecessor had been forced out of office on corruption charges.
Local party leader Wan Qinglian also had to leave.
The crackdown on Guangzhou officials is part of a nationwide campaign against corruption in party and administration, which is being enforced harshly by head of state Xi Jinping.
The new top politician Li Yiwei had worked for an American company before his party career, which is highly unusual in China's Communist Party.
Prior to taking office in Guangzhou, he worked as a top official in Fujian, where the carmaker VW built a large plant.
Frankfurt has officially entered into more than a dozen city partnerships.
The connection to Guangzhou 9000 kilometers away is one of those maintained by both sides, for example with a student exchange.
Last year, the 25th anniversary of German-Chinese connection was celebrated.
The southern Chinese metropolis on the Pearl River is almost as strong with its economic power as the whole of Hesse.
Next year, it wants to reach a gross domestic product of 250 billion euros, Li reported.
Joan Rivers' family "keep their fingers crossed"
Joan Rivers has been unconscious since being admitted to a New York City hospital three days ago; however, her daughter today expressed hope that the 81-year-old comedian is recovering from her illness.
"Thank you for your continued love and support," Melissa Rivers said in a statement today.
We're all keeping our fingers crossed.
Her mother, according to sources, was admitted to Mount Sinai hospital on Thursday following an emergency due to cardiac arrest at a clinic on the Upper East Side, Yorkville Endoscopy.
Doctors keep them intentionally sedated and under close scrutiny until they are more satisfied with their condition, which continues to be described as "serious."
The reactions came from many sides, also in the form of overwhelming online support from Donald Trump, Montel Williams and Kelly Ripa.
Eco-friendly vacuum cleaners and rising wages
Stricter environmental standards will apply in the future for cars and vacuum cleaners, which will go on sale from today.
Similar to refrigerators and washing machines, new vacuum cleaners from September 1 get a label on which the buyer can recognize central data such as the energy efficiency of the device at a glance.
For new cars, stricter nitrogen oxide values apply in the pollutant class "Euro 6".
In addition, there is more money for the scaffolders: They receive a nationwide uniform minimum wage of 10.25 euros.
Amazon buys Twitch for $1.04 billion
AMAZON just confirmed what information had reported on Monday morning: The online retailer - and video producer, and a host of other things - buying video streaming service Twitch for $1.04 billion ($970 million).
The announcement comes as a surprise, not because no one was expecting a takeover of Twitch, but because it was widely suspected that YouTube would acquire the company.
Three months ago it looked as if Twitch's sale to Google's video service was for a cool billion-dollar thing, and the collaboration also appeared natural.
Twitch, which was founded only three years ago as Justin.tv, was launched as a general streaming service - a live version of YouTube.
Instead, it quickly became a platform for players who wanted to release their bravura pieces in games, a "YouTube for live games," according to an insider.
The Twitch interface.
And "let's play," a video genre in which know-it-a-goal (mostly older) games, are already very popular on YouTube.
It’s just that YouTube is often called if you’re trying to describe Twitch, so the news that YouTube wanted to buy Twitch has been picked up a little bored, a textbook example of an established tech company buying up a potential competitor.
It's a mystery why the deal with YouTube didn't come about, as the company was reportedly willing to pay as much as Amazon.
All we know at the moment about this is this statement from Emmet Shear, Twitch's president: "We chose Amazon because they believe in our community, they share our values and long-term visions and they want to help us get there faster."
Another secret is Twitch's incredible success.
For snobs like me, who explain that they prefer to play sports rather than watch, it’s hard to understand the attraction that assumes to watch others play rather than the control console themselves.
It's one thing to look over a friend's shoulder at 3 a.m. as she sneaks through Resident Evil, and something very different to watch a spunner shot in the head 20 times one after the other in Call of Duty.
You could watch all of these games right now.
Another problem is that many of the most popular games today are ego games, so watching without your own control over the viewing perspective can be a really good recipe for headaches.
I'll admit that the fast runs in which the Roger Bannisters of our electronics age complete entire games in record time are entertainingly entertaining.
But as long as there isn't a game or humour virtuoso, his Twitch channel is presumably not very interesting.
But what do skeptics like me know about it?
Twitch is visited by 55 million different visitors per month and is the fourth-largest source of internet traffic spikes.
And how exactly does Amazon want to make money out of it?
It's hard to imagine Twitch being so elegantly incorporated into Amazon Instant Video as YouTube Twitch could have swallowed.
But Twitch has something that any company would like: thousands of young men being courted as advertisers.
As Twitch's chairman, Shear, said Amazon and Twitch "both "believe in the future of gaming," and the medium shows no signs of shrinking, not even - swallowing - as a spectator sport.
There was a lot going on on the bouncy castle at the Burggartenfest on Saturday.
The concept of the organizers was right.
Despite the hefty downpour on Saturday night, an estimated 1200 music fans came to the open-air festival in the Burggarten.
At times the entrance had to be stopped.
"That was annoying," said Ron Sem, one of the organisers.
Salzwedel l light and fun.
From two Spanish words Maxim Sachraj and Ron Sem formed the name Amula.
Because the open air in the Salzwedeler Burggarten should radiate brightness during the day and in the evening through various shows.
Well, the fun comes on its own anyway.
This was already evident early on Saturday, when the first visitors set out for open air.
The organizers had promised a celebration for all generations.
And they succeeded.
Salzwedeler, but also guests from the near and far surroundings had made themselves comfortable around the supply stalls on the Burggartenwiese.
They followed the first live performances, bounced with their feet in beat, enjoyed cool drinks - in short: pure relaxation and enjoyment were announced.
And across the generations.
While some limmed on blankets on the extra-carted white sand at the beach bar, others feasted on a quarter of wine in the vinotheque, the offspring raged on the bouncy castle, which was a great attraction for the smaller guests.
But also during children's make-up, the volunteers literally had their hands full in order to overcome the crowd and conjure up imaginative creations into the little faces.
The bigger ones used the foosball tables or participated in a skateboarding workshop that the Goodfoot-Crew from Salzwedel hosted.
Until the great riain arrived.
Shortly before 17.30 he clashed at the visitors, especially families were looking for the vastness and dryness.
But the attractiveness of the festival did not detract from this.
"There were more people than expected," said Ron Sem, who spoke of a "success across the board."
That’s why there’s nothing wrong with a repeat of the big party next year.
Although the content and size of the event has yet to be worked on, Sem made it clear in a first balance sheet on Sunday compared to the people’s voice.
He described as annoying that in the meantime the admission had to be stopped.
Obviously, the security company had been booked with too few forces.
As a result, some who had already paided no longer came to the premises.
The response was very good.
In a first cautious estimate, the Salzwedel co-organizer spoke of about 1200 visitors tumbling in the castle garden.
There were problems with sound technology.
The Salzwedel hip-hop band Blocks`n`Rox broke off their performance unnerved because of this.
The technicians weren't able to create a reasonably reasonable sound.
The planned hookah lounges could not be set up because the tents could not be brought to Salzwedel because of a puncture.
"But they will be there next year," said the organizer.
The premiere of the Amula-Open-Air was more than successful from the number of visitors.
And that in bad weather.
However, the organizers have committed some mistakes, which will be forgiven for the first time, but have to be eliminated on the second edition.
The organizers also have to take criticism on the social network Facebook.
"Sorry, but for twelve euros you simply expected more," Melanie Meier says on the event page.
"Unfortunately, you have taken over a bit," says Ele Zimmermann in the same place.
Others don't see the initial problems quite as narrow.
I think it’s very good that some not only chat stupidly, but also really try to put something on their feet.
Hats off!" writes Stephanie Jenß to the amula.
Organizers now have a year to put off these criticisms.
New radome in Berkum: Wachtberg's landmark shrinks
That's not the case, as expected.
Rather, the ready-lying component waits to be inserted into the striking structure.
The action is in the context of a larger measure: almost 50 years after the erection observation radar TIRA, the shell, the so-called radome, will be completely renewed.
The new radome will be slightly smaller with a diameter of 47.5 meters than the old one.
However, size still remains a world record.
The last step towards completing the new case is the cap replacement
The upper quarter of the old shell is severed and removed with a crane.
After that, the last quarter of the new shell will be set up.
If the weather plays along, the action should take place at the beginning of this week.
Participant in the Ice Bucket Challenge dislocates jaw
Isabelle Roberts from the UK screamed so much as her ice-cold water was poured over her head that she injured the bone structure of her face.
This ice bucket challenge went painfully wrong.
A woman was hospitalized after screaming at the Ice Bucket challenge so much that she dislocated her jaw.
Isabelle Roberts from the UK shrieked so much as her ice-cold water was poured over her head that she injured her facial structure.
"The water was so cold that I was screaming, and as I did, my jaw just stopped," she told The Mirror.
Ice water is poured over the 20-year-old.
Isabelle Roberts a few seconds before the accident
I wanted to close my mouth, but couldn't do that, the jaw was hooked, and then I understood that something was wrong.
My mother and sister also saw this and almost got into their pants with laughter, but I had to go to the emergency room.
The 20-year-old from the UK has been hospitalised to allow herself to re-adjust the jaw after taking part in Tuesday's viral trend.
The clip has been linked to an internet sensation and a thousand times on Facebook and Twitter.
Will the castle clubs soon pull together again?
Most recently, there was always conflict between the two Wolfratshauser castle clubs.
Now the board of directors are thinking about a possible cooperation.
As a model for the reconstruction of Wolfratshauser Burg, the late Richard Dimbath, former chairman of Wolfratshauser Burgfreunde, led the project in Guédelon, France.
Just a year agove there was not a good climate between the Burgverein and the castle friends Wolfratshausen.
So far, the viewpoints on how to deal with the history of Wolfratshauser Burg and a possible reconstruction have seemed extremely different.
Now the two camps are moving towards each other: Talks about a joint approach are to be held soon.
This is confirmed by the association boards Torsten Sjöberg (Burgverein) and Herbert Piffl (Burgfreunde) at the request of our newspaper.
"I called Mr. Piffl and suggested that we could sit down once," Sjöberg reports.
In his view, all members of both clubs could pull together again in the future.
In this way, more quickly visible successes can be recorded.
While the castle friends around the suddenly deceased Richard Dimbath had until the end a reconstruction of the castle on the mountain forest in mind, for the castle association around Sjöberg the "development and experience" of the site is at the forefront.
Herbert Piffl, second chairman of Burgfreunde, was very happy about the call.
Since Dimbath's death, he has presided over the castle friends.
A new chairman is due to be found at a general meeting in mid-September.
According to the 74-year-old, the club currently counts around 40 members.
"After the death of my friend Richard Dimbath, we initially let the club's activities rest out of respect." This "thinking phase" was necessary to "consider how it could continue."
According to Piffl, the new chairman should then go into the conversation with the Burgverein.
Until then, the Burgfreunde could also make an up-to-date mood within their group.
There is not yet a candidate for the presidency, says Piffl.
"But I'm confident someone's going to find themselves." He himself doesn't want to run because he's staying in Bad Tölz.
The new chairman was supposed to be Wolfratshauser.
The Tölzer wants to "but very happy to continue working on the project".
The different viewpoints regarding reconstruction will probably persist in Piffl’s eyes in the near future.
He pays tribute to Dimbath as a "missionary" who always believed in rebuilding the castle.
Two similar, well-advanced projects in Austria and France would show that this idea is not an aerial castle.
Due to a high participation of the population as well as through volunteers from all over the world, they are a tourist magnet.
Piffl: "Even I was skeptical at the beginning.
But over time over time I have become more and more convinced that it makes sense to advance the project." It can create a "unique selling point" for the rafting city.
There's nothing else here.
In addition, the project would be financeable through EU grants, visitor tickets and potential sponsors, Piffl believes.
"That's why I personally don't want to throw the idea of reconstruction overboard." He hopes that the Burgverein might move something on this point.
Their goal of documenting the story "is important and good".
After that you could look further.
Clever ways to save on university textbooks
Since the cost of university textbooks for many students is more than $1,000 per year, fast-two-semester student Matt Schroeder thought of a clever savings idea.
He invented a lending system for books of higher-semester students and offered nominal compensation to delay resales.
"My math book usually costs $180, but I got it for $10 for a semester and a Chick-fil-A biscuit," said Schroeder, 19, who attends Covenant College in Georgia.
The books needed for the last semester would have cost him $430, he said.
He spent £120.
The college board said students spend on average more than $1,200 annually on textbooks and study materials, but that there were different cost-cutting options for the students.
Online stores and creative approaches like the Schroeders are increasingly the solution of choice for students.
Renting textbooks is becoming more popular, and so students can use a book for a semester, often at a significantly cheaper price than when buying.
Neebo Inc, which operates more than 250 university bookstores, said renting text books had doubled since 2011.
Surveys in the industry show that about a quarter of the books were rented at college bookstores last spring semester, Neebo's vice president Tevor Meyer said.
Less than half of all textbooks are purchased in campus bookstores, according to the National Association of College stores.
Here's the one-off for the best deals.
Buy online
Some new books cost only a third of the price they're available in the campus bookstore online.
The ninth edition of "Calculus" by Ron Larwon, Bruce Edwards and Robert Hostetler costs almost $290, but can be sourced from the specialized textbook retailer Chegg.com for $239.99.
Buying used
If you don’t mind the notes of other or normal signs of use, used books are a good option.
"Calculus" is used on Chegg.com available for $93.49.
Matt Casaday, 25, a graduating student at Brigham Young University, says he spent 42 cents on Amazon.com for a used edition of "Strategic Media Decisions: Understanding The Business End Of The Advertising Business."
The book has been sold for €48.
Academists such as Ingrid Bracey, head of the University of Massachusetts at Amherst College Without Walls, advised students to ask their professors whether previous editions were admissible.
Sometimes the updates are not relevant for the seminar papers.
In such cases, older expenses can often be purchased for a fraction of the cost for the latest edition.
Besides Chegg and Amazon, Half.com from eBay Inc is also a popular alternative for used books.
Rent
Renting textbooks is worth considering if you treat them reasonably well, remember to give them back and don’t want to keep the books after completing the course.
You can save more than 80 percent of the cost of a newly purchased book.
A student, for example, could rent "Calculus" for about $20 for the duration of a semester.
Find out about the provisions of the business at which you are renting, also about the penalty fees that may apply for writing notes, underlinings or traces of use.
Tenant pays attention: If you don’t return the book, the penalty fees may even exceed the price of a new book.
e-books
E-books instead of conventional textbooks are also an option.
Sometimes these books are rented de facto by providing an electronic license for a certain period by wholesalers such as Amazon.com, Chegg and Barnes & Noble Inc.
At Chegg you can rent "Calculus" for 6 months for about $61.
Bracey says students often find the best deals in literature courses, as many classics can be downloaded for free these days, while scientific and engineering textbooks can be very expensive.
Definitely look around.
Joe Gault, 29, who will soon be attending Pepperdine Law School, recommends the ISBN number for price determination on books, as you can be sure to find the right textbook.
And before an online order, one should check, according to Gault, whether the book is actually in stock.
He learned this lesson the hard way.
He bought a book that had four weeks delivery time and eventually paid the full price at the college bookstore.
Why "Guardians of the Galaxy" didn't hit
"The Expendables 3" by Sylvester Stallone grossed less than $30 million of its $90 million budget into the U.S., while "Sin City: A Dame to Kill For" grossed only $12 million of its $70 million budget.
The Cameron Diaz-tailed "Sex Tape" grossed only $14.6 million over the opening weekend, while children's movies like "Dragon Tames Made Easy 2" also disappoint in revenues.
An article by Telegraph film critic Robbie Collin hints that in the children's films, the lack of quality in the films themselves could be the reason.
Collin described 2014 as "the worst year" for children's films so far, describing "dragon taming easily made 2" a purely "functional" fantasy sequel.
However, in the case of films for a slightly older target group, it seems as if the quality of the films is not necessarily the problem.
In July, Entertainment Weekly compared the average CinemaScore and Metacritic rating for every summer film launch shown between American Memorial Day and July 20, 2013 and in the current year on at least 2000 screens.
The results draw on the opinion of moviegoers and film critics and indicate that this year's film hits are equal to those of the past year.
Some commentators say the development is due to changing viewing habits and the growing popularity of web streaming services like Netflix.
Director Jon Favreau, who is currently working on Disney's soon-to-be-release Jungle Book film, told Hollywood Reporter: "I think times are changing."
We have to accept this and not try to follow the past.
In contrast to the currently weak box office results of the Hollywood productions, Netflix recently generated sales of streaming content in the amount of 1.2 billion dollars, almost twice as much as last year, in which sales amounted to $837 million.
Last summer, director Steven Spielberg criticized the film studios for focusing too much on film adaptations of comics, predicting a "nuclear meltdown" of Hollywood.
Viewed in the light of the current downturn, some commentators argue that its bleak prophecy may well contain a true core.
However, due to the potential box office hits expected in the summer of 2015, including Avengers: Age of Ultron, Minions and Jurassic World, other representatives of the film industry are more optimistic.
X-Men's producer Simon Kinberg recently described the decline in box office results as simply "cyclical" and told Hollywood Reporter: "Next summer will be the biggest summer at the box office ever, and nobody's going to worry about the business anymore."
When a few years ago the 2nd table tennis district class was canceled in order to tighten the league division, this has been a step in the right direction.
Now, the German Table Tennis Federation has taken an additional £3 Bundesliga.
As a result, those who did not ascend have descended.
For example, Göttingen's highest-gross team Torpedo has avoided relegation in the Oberliga relegation, but will only play fifth in the coming season.
Torpedo's ladies remain fourth class, despite their terrific march through to the regional league.
Torpedo's boys and girls start in the Lower Saxony League, as well as the boys of SC Weende.
The SG Lenglern and the Torpedo reserve compete in the new Verbandsliga, there are no longer Göttingen clubs represented in the Landesliga.
In the women's, the TTV Geismar plays in the Verbandsliga, the torpedo second in the Landesliga.
What looks like a better district class from the composition is now the new Bezirksoberliga Süd.
TSV Seulingen, VfB Lödingsen, Torpedo III, Geismar, Einbeck, Hammenstedt, Bovender SV, Langenholtensen, FC Weser and TTC compete here.
In the district liga, Dransfeld and the SC Weende allude as regional representatives, in the district class of the TTC II, Rot-Weiß, the Bovender SV II and the teams of the TTV Geismar II and III.
Güntersen, the TTC and the TTV Geismar II are part of the game.
Torpedo III and Sattenhausen/Wöllmarshausen start in the Bezirksliga.
Nine teams each
The SC Weende sends his girls to the small district league.
The district league squadron of the SCW boys - where Torpedo II still starts - is also not complete.
Geismar, Tuspo Weende and Bovender SV go into the season in a district class.
In the first season of the newly founded regional association, the two Göttingen district leagues start with nine teams each.
In the West Squadron are Rot-Weiß II, Bovender SV III, Laubach II, Torpedo IV and V, MTV Grone, Dransfelder SC II, Mielenhausen and SG Lenglern II.
Sattenhausen/Wöllmarshausen, Nikolausberg and Landolfshausen, among others play in the Ost-Staffel.
The women's district league consists of Sattenhausen/Wöllmarshausen II, Bilshausen, Hemeln, Seulingen II, Tuspo Weende and SG Rhume.
This is still better than in the district of Osterode, where there are no women's teams at county level.
It is striking that none of the six 2 district classes reaches target strength, here the innovative and bustling circle board will have to worry.
Nato summit: First protests in Newport and Cardiff
At the weekend, there were protests by opponents of the Nato summit in Newport.
On Saturday, hundreds gathered in the center of Newport for an anti-Nato march.
On Sunday, about 150 people came together in Cardiff in front of the town hall there to hold a so-called countering summit.
Stephen Fairclough haunted the developments over the weekend for us and spoke to protesters who have travelled to Belgium from Bridgend.
Teens flown out of the Blue Mountains
TWO juvenile buschoggers were taken to safety with the winch after being trapped in NSW for the night in the Blue Mountains.
THE 16-year-old girl and 19-year-old man set off for their tour shortly after Sunday noon in Govett's Leap in Blackheath.
Worried relatives called police at about 8 p.m. when the two hadn't returned home.
A search party from local police and ambulance services broke up and the couple were found near the Bridal Veil waterfalls at about 2 p.m.
The girl had injured her knee and the man had fallen and had hit his head.
Rescue workers remained with the couple overnight, and they were flowned out on Monday morning.
They were admitted to Blue Mountains Hospital in stable condition with an ambulance.
Anti-governmental protests in Pakistan escalate
The approximately 1000 protesters had been forced by soldiers from the PTV building, Information Minister Pervaiz Rashid said.
After about half an hour, the station had resumed the broadcast.
PTV director Athar Farooq said protesters armed with sticks had damaged equipment in the editorial office.
The dead or injured were not reported.
The protesters are demanding the resignation of the head of government, whom they accuse electoral fraud.
The police said about a kilometer away from the residence of Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif, there had been clashes of around 3000 protesters with security forces on Monday.
According to the hospital, a police officer was injured.
Over the weekend, at least three people had been killed and more than 500 injured as protesters tried to advance to Sharif's residency.
They are calling for the resignation of the prime minister.
The powerful military had called for a political solution to the conflict on Sunday night after a meeting at army headquarters, "without wasting time and using without violence."
"The army remains committed to its role in ensuring the security of the state."
Escalating violence has stoked concern about an intervention by the military, which has already coupped more frequently in the past.
The protests led by opposition politician Imran Khan and preacher Tahirul Qadri have been going on since Independence Day on August 14.
Sharif opposes a resignation.
Khan and Qadri had called on their supporters on Sunday to persevere.
"We're not going to go back without forcing Sharif to resign," Khan said.
Qadri expressed similar sentiments.
Khan accuses Sharif of having won the general election in May last year through fraud.
Qadri calls for the resignation of central and provincial governments.
Then a transitional government is supposed to reform the electoral system and declare new elections.
More than 40 000 security forces are deployed to protect the government district.
The protesters had initially persevered peacefully outside parliament until Khan and Qadri called on them to advance to Sharif's residency over the weekend.
Hütteldorfer after a second win of the season in third place in the standings.
With the 2:0 over Grödig, a bitter week on Sunday has come to a conciliatory end for Rapid.
"The victory is very liberating," said coach Zoran Barisic, whose team had reached an intermediate low with off in the Europa League on Thursday.
"The sense of success was incredibly important," confirmed captain and goal scorer Steffen Hofmann, who advanced to third place with green-and-white.
Schrammel's dream goal brings Rapid on winner's road Rapid has showned the right reaction to the disappointment against Helsinki with the second win of the season and goes into the international break with a sense of success.
"We wanted to show a positive reaction," said full-back Thomas Schrammel, who set the course for victory with his magnificent leadership volley from the sixteenth corner in the 14th minute.
"I thought I'll hit all the frustration from Thursday and snatched it under the bar," the 26-year-old said with a smile.
When Schrammel scored for the last time in a Bundesliga game on the 13th Novmember in 2010, that was still for Ried.
The first championship goal in the Rapid-Dress will therefore remain in his special memory.
"It's something very special for me, I'm out of the Rapid offspring," Schrammel said.
Due to Mario Pavelic's short-term pelvic injury, Schrammel moved from the left to the right defensive side and did very well there too.
He has enough experience that he can also exercise this position mirror-inverse.
He deserves the goal, he's done some assists this year," Barisic praised.
Team shows character Schrammel also delivered the flank to 2:0 through Hofmann after just over an hour, which finally brought the decision.
Before that, the guests could also have written.
"Thank God we've had some luck today," remarked Schrammel, who alluded to the gap between performance and result in recent weeks: "The results didn't tune, but the play was always good compared to last year."
Barisic even found that "in many games you've been better than it is today" - against "the strongest squad that has played in this stadium this season."
Therefore, the first three-pointer since 26 July proved that the team structure is intact despite the setbacks: "Today my team has showned that it has character".
No less pleasing for him: "That we have finally played to zero again".
A lot of a lot of work during international break Also the fact that Brian Behrendt, Mario Pavelic, Christopher Dibon, Deni Alar and Michael Schimpelsberger have to complain about several failures, and Srdjan Grahovac, Stefan Stangl and Philipp Schobesberger, made the victory even more valuable.
"You can't forget that," warned Barisic, who lost in the past three days with Behrendt and Pavelic players "who have been very stable lately."
The international break must therefore be used to "lick wounds and gain a bit in the physical area," explained Barisic, who has five U21 selection players in his ranks.
Captain Hofmann is one to whom the break seems to be particularly convenient.
He tears himself apart for the team, goes over pain.
Especially at this difficult stage, he has showned that he is a very important player for us," said Barisic.
Hofmann not only gave Rapids to the game before his substitution in the 68th minute but also scored the second goal.
For the 33-year-old, who completed his 350th Bundesliga game, it was the first hit out of the game since 2 November 2013 (4:2 over the Admira).
Hofmann: "Don't believe that everything is good now" "We've certainly had a bit of luck, but at some point, luck has to come back to us as well.
Now it's high time," Hofmann said.
Of course, he urged concentrated further work: "You can't always see this black and white.
We haven't played as badly in recent weeks as we've been portrayed.
However, we must not believe that everything is good now.
He could not dispute the significance of his role for the team.
It’s a very young squad that I’m able to help at the moment.
They stick to me at certain stages," Hofmann said.
Grödig-Coach Michael Baur had little to blame himself and his team.
He regretted the quick counter-hit "out of the magic goal category," as well as Nutz's regular goal in the 40th minute, which had been unjustly revoked by referee Kollegger.
The only accusation that I can make to the team is that we were not concrete enough towards goal in some phases.
We didn’t have enough degrees.
Central defender Maximilian Karner saw it similarly: "It started badly right away, but then we found in and even scored a goal.
By and large, there would have been more in it.
Under this top, peace reigns
We showed a mighty trunk in our puzzle task of the summer series "Mein Stück vom Rhöner Glück" last Friday.
Several of these tree giants still exist in the district, the pictured specimen is the "Geissruheriche" above Unterweißenbrunn.
A bumpy footpath leads up to the 600-year-old oak tree.
It goes past paddocks, basalt stone heaps and basalt stone seas.
A bit below the quarry from Bauersberg, one kilometer north of the road to Weisbach, there it stands.
Vital looks the natural monument, it protrudes beyond the other trees of the surrounding area and is reminiscent of an American sequoia tree with a circumference of over eight meters.
A small Buddha on a tree stump indicates that it is a spiritual or mystical place.
But despite intensive research, there were only references to the origin of the name.
Dr. Wolfgang Schneider consulted the Bischofsheimer Archive and interviewed elderly people from Unterweißenbrunn to find out to find out what kind of knowledge it has with the Geissruhe pond.
The result of his inquiries remained the rather sober explanation for the name.
Until about 80 years ago, the goat herders are said to have driven their animals from Unterweißenbrunn onto the tediously cleared meadows and liberated from stones.
At the foot of the tree are said to have restered the animal keepers exhausted from the ascent.
In the meantime, the area has regained its importance, because on the meadows graze, goat herds are actually grazing again.
The area has also been declared the core zone of the biosphere reserve, Schneider has found out further.
However, caution should be exercised when entering stone accumulations, because the basalt warmed up by the sun is also a popular place to stay for cross otters.
A hiking trail leads from Unterweißenbrunn to the tree, which is somewhat hidden.
Further paths are signposted, ascending towards Hochrhön and inviting you to an extended hike.
Where's the Wasserburg?
However, a water castle, which is searched for in our next task, is rather rarely to be found at altitude.
The reflection in the trench already gives a good impression of the building, but we have some historical buildings in the district, to which the building portrait could fit.
Where is our "Piece of Rhöner Glück"?
my piece Rhöner Glück
As with previous series of the Main-Post in previous years, there are prizes to be won again if they have the right solution.
If you like to take photos yourself, you can also participate.
Under the e-mail address red.neustadt@mainpost.de you can send us the solution and also your very personal "piece of Rhöner Glück" under this keyword.
The most beautiful photos will be published.
After weeks of tug-of-war, Italy's prime minister and incumbent EU chairman, Matteo Renzi, prevailed against strong Conservative resistance at the EU's special summit last week's nomination of his Foreign Minister Federica Mogherini as head of EU's foreign policy. She will succeed Britain's Catherine Ashton.
Renzi considers, as the pro-government daily La Repubblica reproves, the appointment of his Mogherini, who is also considered the favorite in the government team, as strengthening the position of the Social Democrats and his own in the EU.
He also expects a buoyancy for his "reform package" in Italy.
Last but not last, Renzi hopes to calm the left-wing party base, which repeatedly rebels against him, because Mogherini is assigneed to this - albeit rather in a moderate way.
According to the Conservative representatives with Commission President Jean-Claude Juncker and Poland nominated as President of the Council, Donald Tusk, the prevailing opinion in Rome, will create a political balance.
At the same time, the EU's chief diplomat will be deputy to Commission President Juncker.
The still pending approval of the EU Parliament is considered certain.
Otherwise President Giorgio Napolitano would hardly have called the nomination of the 41-year-old politician of the ruling Democratic Party (PD) an "important recognition" of Italy's role in the EU.
La Repubblica spreads optimism that Mogherini, which has been proven for almost two decades in leading party offices and is known for its ambition, will face the "enormous challenges".
The Roman-born woman is married and has two daughters.
Her party career began in 1996 in the youth organization of the then Democratici di Sinistra (Left Democrats), which merged in 2008 with the Catholic center party Margherita to form today's social-democratic PD.
Mogherini became vice-president of the European Community Organization of Socialist Youth (European Young Socialists) for three years.
With the 2001 election to the party council of the Left Democrats, she entered into high party politics, worked in the foreign policy department, of which she soon became.
She occupied traditional priorities of Italian foreign policy such as the Middle East, became known through studies on NATO deployment in Afghanistan, involving Italy with a troop contingent, maintained contacts with the Social Democrats in Europe and with the Democrats in the USA.
Since 2008 she has been a member of the Chamber of Deputies, where she is an expert on defence and European and international affairs.
When Renzi became prime minister in February 2014, he summoned Mogherini to his foreign minister.
If the PD today again has a better relationship with the strong peace movement in the country, which contributed to the over 40 percent votes of the party in the EU elections in May, then Federica Mogherini will have a share in it.
The allegations in Brussels that the new EU foreigner has too little diplomatic experience, i.e. if there is a lack of substance, is countered in the PD.
In Brussels, especially by Poland and the Baltic members, mistruously realistic accents of Mogherini are persecuted on the crisis in Ukraine and defamed as a "Russia-friendly course".
It advocates the South Stream pipeline operated by the Russian company Gasprom, through which Russian natural gas is to be transported directly to southern Europe by circumbenting Ukraine, which also serves the energy security of Italy.
After Renzi took up his term as EU chairman in July, Mogherini traveled to Moscow for her first state visit.
Photographs they showed with Putin at the shake-hands were taken with outrage in Brussels, as well as inviting the Kremlin chief to a Milanese economic summit for October.
A total of nine young people started their training at emz-Hanauer in Nabburg on Monday.
Among them is an apprentice who has already completed a different training in the company.
In the future, the new employees will work in four training occupations: electronics technician (three apprentices), mechatronics technician (two), tool mechanic (two) and process mechanic (two).
All trainees come from the district of Schwandorf: Tobias Braun and Thomas Bücher from Schwarzach, Stefan Brickl and Florian Rewitzer from Schmidgaden, Johannes Beer from Neunburg vorm Wald, Felix Domaier from Oberviechtach, Daniel Sorgenfrei and Sebastian Zahner from Pfreimd and Ferdinand Strebl from Teunz.
The new trainees previously attended secondary or secondary schools and secondary schools.
From Wednesday to Friday, the apprentices of all vintages as well as the instructors go to a team training event in Oberammergau, where, among other things, a full-day hike and rafting on the Inn are on the program.
The company counts 435 employees throughout Germany and 950 worldwide.
The number of apprentices spread over all vintages is 27.
The Socialists in France should "keep their mouths shut up and get France in order," Francois Hollande's main ally said.
Mr Valls called on the deeply divided left to show "their love" for the controversial socialist president, whose cabinet reshuffle has encountered disapproval among the vast majority of the French.
The president deserves everyone's respect, he deserves our loyalty, he deserves our support.
"It's our duty to stay by his side," he said with great applause.
As an appeasing gesture, the prime minister insisted that his government would not question the controversial 35-hour week in France, despite fierce calls for an easing settlement on the part of Emmanuel Macron's new economy minister.
On Saturday, Mr Hollande had urged his co-socialists to appear "unified" with the government.
But Christiane Taubira, the justice minister, sprinkled sand into the gear in which she appeared at a meeting of breakaway socialist lawmakers and criticised the Socialist Party for taking the French "belief in their future."
Marine Le Pen, the far-right leader of the National Front, mocked the socialists' constant trench struggles and said she couldn't imagine that the new Vall's government would endure for more than a few months.
Francois Hollande is the emperor without clothes, but Prince Manuel Valls also has none, they are forced to form a new government when the old one hasn't even survived a summer.
"And the new one won't survive autumn or winter either," Miss Le Pen told supporters.
She reiterated her party's call for a dissolution of parliament and said she was confident of emerging as victorious in early elections and was ready for government.
France's ruling party suffered a defeat in the local elections in March and the right-wing National Front was ahead in May's EU elections.
A poll in Sunday's Journal du Dimanche found that 76 per cent of French people think the Socialist Party faces the danger of disintegrating into several rival factions before the end of Hollande's 2017 term.
Pascal Perrineau, a political scientist at Sciences Po University, warned that the French would quickly lose patience if the new socialist government failed to improve the economy and record unemployment figures.
"There's a small window for possibilities, but public opinion needs to quickly feel that things are changing," he warned.
Otherwise, the situation could deteriorate further.
Norwegian Cruise close to a €3bn prestige cruise deal
Norwegian Cruise Line Holdings Ltd NCHL.O, the world's third-largest crusading provider, is conducting advanced negotiations to acquire rival Prestige Cruises International Inc for about £3 billion, according to connoisseurs.
A deal would give Norwegian Cruise, a $6.8 billion company with a £6.8bn market, access to Prestige Cruises' luxury cruise ships, as well as its prosperous clientele while competing with larger rivals such as Royal Caribbean Cruises Ltd (RCL.N) and Carnival Corp (CCL.N).
An agreement could be announced as early as this week, the sources said on Sunday, warning that the talks, however, could also end unsuccessfully.
Prestige Cruise's owner, private equity firm Apollo Global Management LLC (APO.N), also owns a 20 percent stake in Norwegian Cruise.
The sources asked to remain anonymous, as the negotiations were not public.
Representatives of Norwegian Cruise and Prestige Cruises did not respond to requests for comments, while a spokesperson for Apollo declined to comment.
Miami-based Norwegian Cruise operates 13 cruise ships on routes between North America, the Mediterranean, the Baltic Sea, Central America and the Caribbean.
Its revenues stood at $2.57 billion in 2013, an increase of 13 percent compared to 2012.
Prestige Cruises, also based in Miami, operates under the brand names Oceania and Regent, which together operate eight cruise ships on routes to Scandinavia, Russia, the Mediterranean Sea, North America, Asia, Africa and South America.
It reported revenue for $1.2 billion in 2013, an increase of 6 percent year-on-year.
The cruise industry in total is around $29 billion and is expected for it over the next few years due to strengthening the middle-class in emerging markets such as China and India.
The companies are trying to position themselves for these new customers as the best choice among the cruise operators.
Prestige Cruises applied to U.S. regulators for the IPO in January 2014.
Apollo has been the majority shareholder in the company since an €850 million deal in 2007.
Norwegian Cruise was formed in its current form in 2000 through a merger with a cruise operator owned by Genting Bhd (GENT.KL), a recreational and casino mixed corporation under the control of Malaysian billionaire Lim Kok Thay.
Apollo invested €1 billion in Norwegian Cruise in 2008.
Norwegian Cruise went public in January 2013.
Genting held 28 percent of the shares at the end of June according to licensing authorities, Apollo 20 percent of the shares and the private equity firm TPG Capital LP an 8 percent stake in the company.
Carnival, Royaly Caribbean Cruises and Norwegian Cruise collectively have 82 per cent passenger capacity on American cruise ships, according to the original application documents for Prestige Cruise’s IPO.
Pro-Hong Kong activists condemn China the day after the election decision.
A Beijing-faithful group waved the Chinese flag nearby.
On Sunday, the National People's Congression's Standing Committee passed a framework law allowing only two or three candidates for Hong Kong's leadership election in 2017.
All candidates must first achieve the majority support of a nominating committee, which is expected to be staffed with Beijing-affiliated members.
Through this decision, it becomes almost impossible for opposition Democrats to be admitted to the election, prompting pro-democracy activists to carry out their threat and paralyze Hong Kong's financial center with "Occupy Central" protests.
Political reforms have long been a contentious issue in Hong Kong; and China's party leadership fears that calls for democracy will spread to other cities.
Following the publication of a Beijing White Paper outlining China's supremacy over Hong Kong in June, democracy activists held an unofficial referendum on the electoral procedure in the special administrative region where hundreds of thousands marched into the city's business district and organised a sit-in there.
Li's survey is organized by the Hong Kong government and the Chinese liaison office in Hong Kong.
The Vice-Chairman of the Legal Commission of the Standing Committee, Zhang Rongshun and the Vice-Director of the Office of Hong Kong and Macau of the State Council, Feng Wei, were to be questioned over the course of the day.
Student activists announced they would gather in the afternoon in front of Hong Kong's senior's office.
Until the final phase of the 150-year colonial government in Hong Kong, Britain did not envisage any democratic elements.
Putin asks Kiev to start 'state affairs talks' with eastern Ukraine
According to Russian media reports, Russian President Putin demanded on Sunday that the Ukrainian government stop the fights against the separatists in the east of the country and immediately start negotiations on "statehood" of the breakaway region.
His spokesman Dimitry Peskov later clarified that Putin did not mean that the territory of eastern Ukraine under the control of the separatists would become part of Russia, but that his status to Ukraine had to be redefined in order to give the Russian-speaking region the power to represent its rights and interests.
Putin's call to the Kiev government to negotiate on an equal footing with the pro-Russian insurgents, however, fits the strategy he apparently pursued since the riots began five months ago: aid for the separatists in taking territories and pressure on the Ukrainian government to allow the newly proclaimed Novorossiya region factual independence for an alliance with Russia rather than with the West.
In an interview with state-run Channel One television, Putin condemned Ukraine's military campaign to recapture separatist-held areas in the eastern regions of Donetsk and Luhansk, which were taken in March and April after Moscow annected Ukraine's Crimean peninsula on March 18.
The Kremlin and the separatists have lately referred to the area taken as "Novorossiya" or "Neurusia," a term reminiscent of the pre-revolutionary heyday of the Russian Empire.
Putin pointed out that all those who, in the wake of Ukrainian politicians' campaigns for the October 26 elections, and while government groups are attacking civilian communities in the areas held by separatists, hoping for soon-to-be peace talks "captured in their illusions," Itar-Tass reported.
"We must immediately start extensive discussions, not only on technical issues, but also on the political organization of society and the state status of south-eastern Ukraine to safeguard the interests of the people living there," he said.
Peskov said Putin's reference to statehood needed to be seen in the context of the greater autonomy, which has been debated by the Kiev leadership for months to dispel fears in Russian-speaking areas that their cultural and linguistic rights were at risk.
Only the Ukrainian government can grant necessary autonomy to the eastern territories, Peskov said.
This is not about negotiations between Ukraine and Russia, Peskov said, "because it's not about a conflict between Russia and Ukraine, it's about an internal conflict in Ukraine."
Kremlin spokesman's clarification on correcting the "misinterpretation" of Putin's remarks underscored Russia's claim to leadership in dealing with separatist rebellion in the East as opposed to Crimea's direct annection, in which the majority of the 2 million residents are ethnic Russians.
Moscow would have far greater difficulty in annexing the Donetsk and Luhansk regions in eastern Ukraine, since most of the 6.5 million inhabitants are not Russians and polls from the pre-conflict period yielded a broad majority to remain in Ukraine.
The autonomy approached by Russian diplomats on international forums would give regional governments in Ukraine authority to negotiate their own trade agreements and external relations, giving the Kremlin de facto control over an area linking Russian territory with Crimea.
The peninsula annexed five months ago in the Black Sea is the home port for the majority of the Russian navy as well as an important trading port and historic coastal region.
Moreover, the regions between the Rostov region in Russia and Crimea is rich in mines, factories and foundries that produce important components for the Russian military.
Ukrainian President Petro Poroshenko suggested during his inauguration speech on June 7 that Ukraine's legislation - after new elections - should consider additions to the constitution that give the respective territories greater control over their finances and the status of languages.
But his notion of autonomy appears to be in sharp contrast to those of the Kremlin and separatist rebels, whose arming and incitement Moscow is accused.
Putin's last call for Kiev to negotiate with the separatist leaders on an equal footing followed after a further advance by the rebels last week after Russian troops and tanks invaded eastern Ukraine over the previously peaceful region along the Sea of Azov.
The Russian-backed separatists took control of Novoazovskovsk in a swipe, according to fears of Ukrainian security officials.
For this reason, massive civilian and military preparations have been underway to fortify Mariupol, a 500,000-inhabited steel industrial port located between Novoazovsk and narrow access to the Crimean peninsula.
Poroshenko spoke at an EU leadership meeting in Brussels on Saturday and urged for action to prevent further Russian aggression against Ukraine, a former Soviet republic that has been independent for 23 years.
"We are on the verge of a point where there is no turning back," Poroshenko warned.
Thousands of foreign troops and hundreds of foreign tanks are now located on the territory of Ukraine.
The EU summit decided no concrete actions, the leadership demanded the draft of further sanctions against Russia, which are to come into force in an unspecified escalation of the Ukraine crisis.
Ukrainian soldiers had to withdraw from their positions in Ilovajsk after two Russian tank columns and 1,000 troops invaded the Donetsk region last week to support the besieged separatists, Colonel Andrij Lysenko, spokesman for Ukraine's National National Security and Defense Council, told reports in Kiev on Saturday.
The first presumably 63 Ukrainian soldiers trapped by the Russian invasion of Ilovaysk were exchangeded on Sunday for 10 Russian paratroopers who had been captured on Ukrainian territory a week ago, Lysenko announced on Sunday.
Nude photo scandal over Australian celebrities and Gabi Grecko
Geoffrey Edelsten made no secret of his annoyance over hackers who presumably stole nude pictures of his fiancée Gabi Grecko and a who of the A-proms from Hollywood.
The film city is in excitement after a series of clear photos of a nude Jennifer Lawrence surfaced in a hacking scandal surrounding celebrities on the internet.
The hacker arguably has 60 nude photos of Lawrence, star from "the Tributes of Panem" and superstars, including models Kate Upton and Cara Delevingne, singers Rihanna, Ariana Grande and Lea Michelle, as well as actress Kirsten Dunst.
Australian actresses Teresa Palmer, Emily Browning, Yvonne Strahovski, and Grecko from Melbourne reportedly leaked private photos due to an iCloud gap.
There are 101 celebrities on the list.
Edelsten, who got engaged to Grecko last month, told Confidential: "It's disgusting."
All private letters and pictures should remain private.
It's a shame that private information can be stolen and passed on to others.
Grecko, currently in New York, reportedly told a news website that the hack was "disgracing" and that those affected felt "hurt."
The hacker presumably has 30 pictures of Palmer with her ex-boyfriend Scott Speedman, including two shots in which she lies on top without a pool.
Palmer's film works include "Love is Invincible," starring Liam Hemsworth.
Palmer, Chuck star Strahovski and actress Browning from Sucker Punch weren't available for comments on the chopped pictures yesterday.
A representative told TMZ: "It's a vile violation of privacy."
Actor Seth Rogen condemned the hacker in his tweet: "Posting hacked images from a mobile phone is nothing more than the sale of stolen goods."
From a purely legal point of view, the dissemination of stolen images should not be tolerated.
Cyclists, joggers and hikers were there yesterday at the 8th cycling on the Großer Feldberg and have thus supported the association "Help for children with cancer".
The tour, which was an ordeal for some, represented a light workout for the others.
In one, however, everyone agreed: it was fun.
Thomas Studanski may have slept well last night.
For the first time in his life, the head of the Oberurseler Brauhaus cycled to the Feldberg yesterday.
for a good cause.
He can easily put away the small sore muscles, especially since the participation in the 8th cycling on the Großer Feldberg in favor of the association "Hilfe for cancer-sick children" (Frankfurt) has also personally brought him a valuable insight.
That's a great thing.
“I now know that I can do this – great!”
Small break on the way in the applause curve?
No, if I'd stopped, it'd have been over...
Like Thomas Studanski, who sits for the CDU in the Oberursel city parliament, many of the 718 participants tortured themselves yesterday in early thaw and came close to exhaustion on the plateau.
Others, on the other hand, found it easier to overcome the eleven-kilometre route between Hohemark and Feldberg.
Norman Dießner, for example.
The completely in red Green politician from the Kreistag - red dress, red bike - did something at the start on understatement ("A lot of professionals, there's arriving the goal!"), but then turned out to be half a professional on the mountain and went through the finish after 45 minutes without shortness of breath.
He, too, drove through the track in a slide, as did Nina Kuhn, press spokeswoman for the city of Oberursel, who had set out together with Frank Grüneisen.
For the two triathletes, "Feldberg cycling" was not necessarily a sporting challenge: "This is about charity, we are happy to participate - we would have made an exit today anyway," says Kuhn.
On the way they had met Paul Wenzel from Oberreifenberg.
The boy (12) had gone down to Oberursel in the morning to ramp back on the height.
Frankfurt's Florian Zierz had swung into the saddle at home at 9 a.m. in the morning, "made" the Feldberg twice, then rolling home with fun.
"Fry Toni" also had fun.
For the boss of the Oberurseler Kultkiosk, cycling tours over the Feldberg are simply part of it.
He had been aiming for 40 minutes with his 6.5-kilo racing bike.
It's a crazy event.
For this purpose - I'm happy to be there," said Andreas Balzke.
The well-trained Mainzer says of himself that he is an "ambitious road cyclist".
It has to be, because he was on the road with the "Fixie wheel", these are also racing bikes, but without a gearshift...
Not only the 20 hikers, who climbed over the White Wall and the fox dance to the summit, but also a jogger who pulled a car tire with guets behind him on a cord, proved that it went loosely upwards.
Bergwatt doctor Professor Volker Lischke, who provided safety with his team, equipped with four-wheel-drive bully and quad, said: "I know him from Frankfurt, who trains for a special sleigh trail, except that he pulls the sled himself".
The 718 participants - and thus as many as never before - had been sent to the track in groups in the morning.
The hikers first at 9.30 a.m.
This was followed by the first cyclists and runners at 10 a.m.
Five minutes later, the first mountain bikers started.
Bent Hansen, chairman of the association "Radeln auf den Großen Feldberg", gave the starting squads and wished the participants a nice trip.
For next year, he hopes that it will be possible to close the route to Feldberg up to the safety of the participants.
